        "handicrafts": [
          {
            "id": "zyy7utnh0",
            "title": "Woodcraft",
            "name": "Woodcraft",
            "desc": "Wood inlay is done with different materials, metal, ivory and stag-horn. Apart from decorative pieces like wall hangings, table tops, trays, a number of utility articles are also ornamented with inlay work. The designs are mostly geometrical, very fine and colourful.",
            "img": "/state-images/Bihar/wood-inlay.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "u8m6hxbxm",
            "title": "Bihar \u0096 Handicrafts -Lacquerware",
            "name": "Bihar \u0096 Handicrafts -Lacquerware",
            "desc": "Lac has been gathered and used in Bihar for ages. The vermilion container called sindurdan used in marriages by brides is made by a community called laheris. The boxes are decorated with the set motifs of fish, chakra and peacock which have a moving rythmic quality. Bangles of lac decorated with beads and glass are commercially produced in Bihar.",
            "img": "https://www.indianeagle.com/travelbeats//wp-content/uploads/2014/05/madhubani-painting-of-bihar-india.jpg",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "6jkgxx6mo",
            "title": "Stonecraft",
            "name": "Stonecraft",
            "desc": "The ancient sculptures of the Mauryan period are witness to Bihar's rich heritage of stonecraft. An outstanding example is the famous Asoka pillar at Sarnath. The most noted stoneware centre of Bihar is Patharkatti in Gaya district. This area is famous for the blue black pot stone from which images and household articles like the pestle or the mortar kharal (medicine grinder) are made. Buddhist sculptures are also made out of this stone. Chandil and Karaikalla in Singhbum district and Dumka in Santhal parganas are famous for the beautifully grained greenish black soapstone.",
            "img": "/state-images/Bihar/kharal.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "lqkz00q49",
            "title": "Bihar \u0096 Handicrafts -Masks",
            "name": "Bihar \u0096 Handicrafts -Masks",
            "desc": "Masks are used for the Chhou dance of Saraikela in Bihar. The Masks are not only very stylish but also equally delicate. The most distinguishing features of the masks are the stylized arch-like eyebrows and elongated half closed eyes. The stylized gestures of the eyebrows, eyes and mouth are painstakingly painted to bring out the distinctive character. These features impart a sense of mystery to the mask. Traditionally only those people who were dancers were permitted to make the masks. Originally they were made of wood, later of bamboo, then pumpkin shells and now papier-ma\u0302che\u0301. These masks are painted mainly with flat transparent shades and the lines are bold and clear. Avoiding the realistic identification the masks take up a symbolic character.",
            "img": "/state-images/Bihar/seraikela-mask.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "o3ca8pc1c",
            "title": "Bihar \u0096 Handicrafts - Printed Textiles",
            "name": "Bihar \u0096 Handicrafts - Printed Textiles",
            "desc": "Printing is done on cotton, wool and silk at Bhagalpur, Bihar Sharif, Darbhanga, and Patna. Gaya, being a famous place of pilgrimage has the religious textiles with the names or footprints of deities printed all over in ochre or red. The chunris of Bihar have designs ranging from traditional to floral and animal forms. There is a small printing sector in North Bihar at Sursand where only mica (khari) printing is done. Bright colours are used and largely dots and stars strewn all over form the designs.",
            "img": "/state-images-new/Bihar/87d70ff74d282a7f48cd1a743eeae864.jpeg",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
