        "dance_forms": {
          "folk": [
            {
              "id": "791bd797",
              "name": "Jhumar Dance",
              "desc": "Jhumar is a traditional folk dance of Bihar, which is performed by the rural women. There is no fixed season for this beautiful dance form, it is a dance, which is performed at all times. Spring descends on earth with its beauty and spreads joy and happiness all around. The women dance with gay abandon. The men folk are invited to join in and they usually provide the musical accompaniment.",
              "img": "/downloaded_images/ece3a73e410ba8b011c3e7122607eb79.webp",
              "artists": ""
            }
          ],
          "classical": []
        }
      },
      "monuments": [
        {
          "id": "dlmzagngz",
          "title": "Bihar- Monument- Sasaram",
          "name": "Bihar- Monument- Sasaram",
          "desc": "Sher Shah Suri of the Suri dynasty of Bihar fought with Humayun in Delhi. His mausoleum was built in the middle of a lake in Sasaram by his architect, Aliwal Khan. The tomb is an imposing structure of stone standing and rising from a large stone terrace. This terrace rests obliquely on a platform with a flight of steps leading to the water\u0092s edge. The upper terrace is enclosed by a battlemented parapet wall with octagonal domed chambers at four corners. In the middle of the upper terrace stands the building of the mausoleum on a large octagonal chamber surrounded by a wide verandah on all the four sides. Internally, the verandah is covered by a series of 24 small domes. The roof is a pillared cupola adorned by panels of white glazed tiles now much discoloured. The interior of the tomb is sufficiently well ventilated and lighted through large windows on the top portion of the walls fitted with stone jali in varying patterns. The jambs and spandrils of the arch of the mihrab on the western wall were once profusely adorned with verses from the Quran and inscriptions, with glazed tiles of various colours arranged in geometrical patterns and with beautiful floral carvings in stone enclosed in enamel borders. Much of this decoration has already vanished.",
          "img": "/state-images/Bihar/sher-shah-tomb.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 1500-1550",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 24.9509656,
          "lon": 84.0148733
        },
        {
          "id": "y6538pqc9",
          "title": "Bihar- Monument- Rajgir",
          "name": "Bihar- Monument- Rajgir",
          "desc": "Rajgir, known earlier as Rajagriha or Girivaraja, located in the rocky hills is significant for the teachings of both Buddha and Mahavira. Rajgir is 10km south of Nalanda and sacred to the memory of the founder of both Buddhism and Jainism. Lord Buddha spent many months of retreat during the rainy season here, and used to meditate and preach on Giddhkuta, the Hill of the Vultures. Lord Mahavir spent fourteen years of his life at Rajgir and Nalanda. It was in Rajgriha that Lord Buddha delivered some of his famous sermons and converted a number of people to Buddhism including King Bimbisara of Magadha. Today, Rajgir is a picturesque and serene place, visited by pilgrims from all over. Venuvana is the site of the monastery Venuvana Vihar built by King Bimbisara for Lord Buddha to reside. This was the King's first offering to Lord Buddha. Swarna Bhandar are cave chambers which were hollowed out of a single massive rock. One of the chambers is believed to have been the guard room, the rear wall has two straight vertical lines and one horizontal line cut into the rock; this doorway is supposed to lead to King Bimbisara\u0092s treasury. Inscriptions in the Sankhalipi or shell script, etched into the wall and so far undeciphered, are believed to give the clue to open the doorway. The treasure, according to folklore, is still intact. The second chamber bears a few traces of seated and standing guards etched into the outer wall.",
          "img": "/state-images/Bihar/rajgir.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Ancient 2500-600 BCE",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 25.0299973,
          "lon": 85.4207368
        },
        {
          "id": "dae91lhqf",
          "title": "Bihar- Monument - Vaishali",
          "name": "Bihar- Monument - Vaishali",
          "desc": "Vaishali is significant to both Buddhists and Jains. Lord Buddha visited Vaishali more than once during his lifetime. He preached his last sermon here and announced his approaching Mahaparinirvana to his followers. Hundred years after he attained Mahaparinirvana, it was the venue of the second Buddhist council held at the time of king Kalasoka. Two stupas were erected to commemorate this event. Lord Mahavir was born on the outskirts Vaishali at a place called Kundupur in 527 BCE and lived in there till he was 22. Vaishali remains an important pilgrimage for Buddhists and Jains.To commemorate the importance of the site, Emperor Ashoka erected one of his famous pillars. It is made of a highly polished single piece of red sandstone, surmounted by bell shaped capital. A life-size figure of a lion surmounts the top of the pillar. There are some important Stupas here. The Buddha Stupa-I has a plain surface.The exterior of this stupa is now in a dilapidated condition One-eighth of the sacred ashes of the Lord Buddha were enshrined here in a stone casket. Excavation at this site in 1958 led to the discovery of Buddha Stupa-II with remains of another casket containing the ashes of the Lord Buddha.",
          "img": "/state-images/Bihar/vaishali-bihar.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Ancient 600-001 BCE",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 25.7491703,
          "lon": 85.3972164
        },
        {
          "id": "bbrug8ip4",
          "title": "Bihar- Monument- Bodhgaya",
          "name": "Bihar- Monument- Bodhgaya",
          "desc": "The founder of Buddhism, Gautama Buddha, attained enlightenment under the sacred Bodhi tree or Banyan tree at Bodhgaya. Bodhgaya is one of the four most sacred pilgrimages for the Buddhists. The other three sacred places of Buddhists are Lumbini (Buddha's place of birth), Sarnath (the site of his first sermon) and Kushinagar (place where he left his body). Buddhists from all over the world visit the holy site of Bodhgaya. They have also built many Buddhist temples at Bodhgaya. The Mahabodhi Temple is located where Lord Buddha got enlightenment. The present Mahabodhi temple was restored around 1880 CE. Besides the main Mahabodhi temple there are several other temples at the place. In the inner sanctuary of the temple there is a huge statue of Lord Buddha. In the center of the temple there is a Shivalinga that was installed around 860 CE. It was under the Bodhi tree that Buddha attained enlightenment. A sapling of the original banyan tree was carried by Ashoka's daughter Sanghamitra to Sri Lanka and planted at Anuradhapura where it exists even now. A cutting from the Bodhi tree of Anuradhapura was planted at Bodhgaya when the original tree died. Vajrasana (the seat of stability) under the bodhi tree is believed to be the same seat where Buddha sat, during his meditation. The stone platform, under the Bodhi tree symbolizes the Vajrasana.",
          "img": "/state-images/Bihar/bodhgaya1.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Ancient 3rd Centuary BCE",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 24.677623,
          "lon": 84.9727292
        },
        {
          "id": "azo8m8e9x",
          "title": "Bihar- Monument- Barabar Hill",
          "name": "Bihar- Monument- Barabar Hill",
          "desc": "The Lomas Rishi Cave was a unique architectural development during Emperor Ashoka (third Cen BCE). This is one of the seven cave temples on the Barabar and Nagarjuna hills belonging to a religion known as Ajivika which was prevalent in those days.These caves are small and simple,with",
          "img": "/state-images/Bihar/lomas-rishi.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Ancient 600-001 BCE",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 25.0055964,
          "lon": 85.0633016
        },
