          }
        ],
        "handicrafts": [
          {
            "id": "rmw1g9pkg",
            "title": "Chhattisgarh \u0096 Handicrafts - Bamboo Craft",
            "name": "Chhattisgarh \u0096 Handicrafts - Bamboo Craft",
            "desc": "Chhattisgarh has a very wide variety of bamboo grown in the state and the craftspersons of the state make excellent bamboo items. Articles for daily as well as decorative use are produced by the local tribal artisans. Some of the well known bamboo products of the state include agricultural implements, fishing traps, hunting tools and baskets.",
            "img": "",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "6x4h69w4r",
            "title": "Chhattisgarh \u0096 Handicrafts - Woodwork",
            "name": "Chhattisgarh \u0096 Handicrafts - Woodwork",
            "desc": "The craftspeople of Chattisgarh make beautiful decorative wood carvings. The skillful craftsmen of the state carve beautiful wooden ceilings, doors, lintels etc using different kinds of wood like shisham, teak, dhudi, sal and kikar. The craftsmen also make pipes, masks, doors, window frames and sculptures.",
            "img": "https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/1194/1498/files/b6_88f218a7-0098-4cb8-87b9-acf4e273616e_480x480.webp?v=1716196956",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "i7rtnpow4",
            "title": "Metalcraft",
            "name": "Metalcraft",
            "desc": "Bastar is inhabited by the most exotic tribes of India. It is richly endowed with minerals and forest resources. Bastar is, predominantly, a forest area, and home of Marias and Murias, both extremely colorful and picturesque tribes, who have preserved their ancient culture. The lohars of Kondagaon village in Bastar have been engaged in iron craft for generations and indulge in making of farm implements, lamps and other household objects. The iron objects are made by hammering them into shape; they are not cast or moulded. The hot iron is beaten repeatedly to the desired shape while the unnecessary portion is cut away and filed to remove the sharp edges. Hollow images usually bigger than solid ones are made out of beaten iron sheets, folded, cut, and filed to shape the deepak (lamps), and vertical and horizontal rods or strips. These are ornamented with bird and animal figures that are made separately and later joined together into various types of composite lamps. Depending on the variations in structure and use, there are five types of lamps: laman diya, supali diya, gadli diya, khut diya and viman diya.",
            "img": "/state-images/Chhattisgarh/bastar-lohar.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "vpggu2061",
            "title": "Chhattisgarh \u0096 Handicrafts - Dhokra work",
            "name": "Chhattisgarh \u0096 Handicrafts - Dhokra work",
            "desc": "Bastar and Raigarh districts of Chhatisgarh create Dhokra crafts from bell metal, brass and bronze. Dhokra essentially refers to the casting of bell metal or bass using the lost-wax technique. In Chhattisgarh, it is the Ghadwa community that is associated with this craft. In local etymology, Ghadwa means \u0091to shape or create\u0092. A variety of products are crated by the Ghadwas for local use such as images of local gods and deities, vessels, and jewellery. The method of lost wax procedure or cire perdue is used to cast bronze articles. carpenters. Mutni, sooja, pinachaku, hatwani, chimta are few of the tools. Though Dhokra casting is used across the globe, the \u0091coiled thread technique\u0092 is unique to Bastar. The traditional lost wax technique is simple and ideal for use in tribal settings. The craftsman begins by winding a slim thread of wax over the contours of a clay core. It is then thickly coated with fine clay obtained from termite bills, and baked on drying, leaving a narrow vent to melt away the wax. The vacuum created between the core and the clay layer is filled with molten metal, which is then allowed to cool down and solidify. Then the outer clay mould is cracked open, revealing the beauty of the final sculpture. Simple as this whole process seems, it requires great precision and skill. The metal must be able to flow uniformly and freely through the narrow spaces, and replace the wax without forming any bubbles or gaps. Cow dung, paddy husk and red soil are also used in the manufacture of Dhokra artifacts. Of all the raw materials used by the Ghadwas, the most important is beeswax. Besides the essential contouring, wax wires and pieces are also used for decorations required for finishing the artifacts. It is used because of its extraordinarily high plastic content and pliability. Examples of lost wax casting are practiced all over the world, but the coiled thread technique is unique to Bastar. A glance at the traditional baskets provides a clue to its origin. The basket makers would wind grass around a rope, which was then coiled into shape. The same technique was translated into metal only much later, with forest dwellers being dependent on natural product long before they began to use metal. Metal anklets with basket weave motifs, and beautiful containers reminiscent of wicker baskets also point towards such a transition.",
            "img": "/state-images/Chhattisgarh/Dhokra.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "okz9k6za6",
            "title": "Chhattisgarh \u0096 Handicrafts - Silk Weaving",
            "name": "Chhattisgarh \u0096 Handicrafts - Silk Weaving",
            "desc": "Chattisgarh produces excellent quality of silks. Raigarh and Champa are the main silk producing centres.The craftspersons perform traditional and contemporary work. Embroidery is the main attraction on the silk fabrics and there are stitches which are as old as 150 years. Marabhai is one of the traditional embroidery which maintains all its traditional stitches and it\u0092s a form of hand embroidery. Kosa and Ahimsa are the other most commonly used. The silk thread for the weave is obtained from the jungles of Chhattisgarh without killing the silkworms and involves using crushed and dried Mahuwa seeds to trap the silkworms after the formation of the cocoons.",
            "img": "https://lookaside.instagram.com/seo/google_widget/crawler/?media_id=3338084308704547250",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
