    {
      "id": "delhi",
      "name": "Delhi",
      "geography": {
        "capital": "New Delhi",
        "key_cities": []
      },
      "facts": [
        "Joseph Allen Stein was born, in the United States of America. After studies and practice in San Francisco he moved to India in 1952, and became head of the department of architecture at the Bengal Engineering College in Calcutta. Several other foreign architects arrived in India about the same time, including the French architect Le Corbusier, who designed the city of Chandigarh, the Americans Louis Kahn and Charles Eames, who were working on projects at Ahmedabad. But unlike others, Mr. Stein stayed on and practiced architecture in New Delhi from 1955 until his retirement in 1995. Mr. Stein's best-known Indian creations were probably the Triveni Kala Sangam arts complex, a collection of buildings on Lodi Estate in New Delhi, including headquarters of the Ford Foundation, UNICEF and the World Wide Fund for Nature, the India International Center, and the India Habitat Center. Distinguished by their elegant formal spaces, beautifully muted covered walkways, extensive planting and landscaping and their meticulous detailing make them great examples of modern Indian architecture.",
        "**2000- Contemporary**: Nicknamed \u0093India\u0092s Spiritual Theme Park\u0094, the Swaminarayan Akshardham complex is one of the finest structures build in the modern India. The Akshardham Temple\u0092s main monument is constructed of pink stone and white marble having two-hundred-and-thirty carved pillars, nine ornate domes, a magnificent base of carved elephants, and twenty-thousand statues of saints. The gardens, or Bharat Upvan, consist of lush lawns and manicured gardens, with sixty bronze statues of different historical and mythological subjects."
      ],
      "art_forms": {
        "paintings": [
          {
            "id": "2xko3xkcs",
            "title": "Visual of Company Painting",
            "name": "Visual of Company Painting",
            "desc": "The city was planned systematically, combining twentieth century architecture with that of twentieth centuries older. Sir Edward Lutyens was responsible for the overall plan of New Delhi, and his forte is Rajpath, approached by a 3.2km long road flanked by the imposing buildings of the two Secretariats, built by Herbert Baker. Once known as The Viceroy Palace the Rashtrapati Bhawan, built of brown stone, is truly a befitting home for the President of India and remains Lutyens most significant achievement. It is unquestionably a masterpiece of symmetry, discipline, silhouette, colour and harmony flanked by the two Secretariats and the three buildings together, opens into a huge square called the Viceroy\u0092s Court where the Jaipur Column stands tall. The Viceroys Court, which frames the main entrance to the house, has lateral entrances on the axis of the Jaipur Column. The main entrance is approached by a broad flight of steps which lead to a 12-column portico. The most outstanding feature of the building is the huge copper dome that rises over a vast colonnaded facade. Beneath the dome is the circular Durbar Hall. The coloured marbles used in the hall come from all parts of India. The columns at the front entrance have bells carved into their capitals. To the west the Bhawan overlooks an enormous Mughal garden designed by Lutyens. Here the principles of hierarchy, order, symmetry and unity are extended from the house into the landscape. A series of ornamental fountains, walls, gazebos and screens are combined with scores of trees, flowers and shrubs. The seat of Indian Parliament, the Sansad Bhawan is a marvellous piece of architecture designed by Lutyens and constructed under the direct supervision of Sir Herbert Baker. Designed as a circular structure, the two semicircular house chambers flank the Central Hall with its impressive dome. The building has a continuous open corridor on the first floor fringed with a colonnade of 144 creamy sandstone pillars. The exterior walls of red sandstone are carved in geometric patterns that echo Mughal jaalis. But it wasn\u0092t Lutyens or Bakers who built the rest of New Delhi as it is commonly believed; most if its structures were designed by an unknown Englishman called Robert Tor Tussell, who built Connaught Place, Eastern and Western Courts, Flagstaff House (where Jawaharlal Nehru lived later) and the thousands of public buildings, post offices, officer\u0092s bungalows and public buildings. India Gate, erected as a memorial in the memory of 90,00 soldiers who laid down their lives during world war I is located at Rajpath, the gateway is 42 m high. Also known as the All India War Memorial, India Gate was designed and constructed by Lutyens. Another additional 13,516 names engraved on the arch and foundations form a separate memorial to the British and Indian soldiers killed on the North-West Frontier in the Afghan War of 1919. Another memorial, Amar Jawan Jyoti is in the form of a flame that burns day and night under the arch to remind the nation of soldiers who perished in the Indo-Pakistan War of December 1971. The entire arch stands on a low base of red Bharatpur stone and rises in stages to a huge cornice.",
            "img": "/state-images/Delhi/rashtrapati-bhavan.webp",
            "historical_significance": "1900-1947 Modern",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
