      "monuments": [
        {
          "id": "izan9ko7h",
          "title": "Delhi-Monument-Qutab Complex",
          "name": "Delhi-Monument-Qutab Complex",
          "desc": "1100-1200 Early Medieval The Qutub Minar, probably a tower of victory started by Qutub-ud-din Aibak during the twelfth century, and completed by Altamash is an interesting mix in Hindu and Islamic styles and each of the original three storeys has different designs. The base storey has alternate angular and circular extensions while those of the second one are round and the third one has only angular flutings. The projecting balconies have a very interesting pattern with an intricate design in which triangular pieces of vaulting spring from the corners. The attractiveness of the balconies is heightened by the bands of deep inscriptions. The complex also houses the Quwwat-ul-Islam Masjid which was erected on the site of a Hindu temple. It has an intricately carved ceiling followed by the spectacular courtyard of the mosque which is lined by the rows of the profusely adorned pillars on both sides. Hindu motifs, like tasseled ropes, bells, tendrils, cows and leaves, are visible all over the mosque. The very first indication of the Islamic character of the building come from the elegant pointed arches with curvaceous and serpentine calligraphy of texts from Quran in Arabic crowning them, towards the west of the mosque. A massive stone screen erected in front of the prayer hall, with a central arch and two similar, though smaller, arches on either side; are profusely carved, crowded with exquisite decorations and is one of the earliest and finest examples of the fusion of Hindu and Islamic art. Alai Darwaza, is the first manifestation of Islamic, architecture and ornamentation has a huge dome with a central knob, pointed arches shaped like horseshoes, and the \u0091lotus-bud\u0092 fringes of the arches. The Darwaza is built of red sandstone with arched openings on all sides. The arch frames are embellished with \u0091lotus-bud\u0092 designs, which are also seen in the perforated screens on the sides of the gate.The gateway is very skillfully decorated withcomplex carvings of the geometric patterns and white marble bands of calligraphy. Apart from Qutab minar, the area also hosts many tombs such as Jamali Kamali Tomb, Sultan Garhi Tomb, Balban Tomb, Madhi Masjid, Palam Mosque and many more.",
          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/d_007.webp",
          "historical_significance": "1100-1200 Early Medieval",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 27.62166231496951,
          "lon": 76.93626395971958
        },
        {
          "id": "o5fbq5hbh",
          "title": "Delhi - Monument - Ferozeshah Kotla",
          "name": "Delhi - Monument - Ferozeshah Kotla",
          "desc": "Feroze Shah Kotla, the palace of Feroze Shah Tughlaq has nothing much that survives, except the Ashoka Pillar, which Feroze Shah Tughlaq had brought from Topra, in Ambala and the ruins of the Jama Masjid and a round step well (baoli), which is two floors deep and has antechambers at each level.",
          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/d_010.webp",
          "historical_significance": "",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 29.376093069973184,
          "lon": 77.96213121576386
        },
        {
          "id": "pyd5i71if",
          "title": "Delhi \u0096 Monument - Red Fort",
          "name": "Delhi \u0096 Monument - Red Fort",
          "desc": "1700-1750 Medieval Shahjahan laid out a new walled city called Shahjahanabad (the present old Delhi) along the Yamuna. It is there he erected a Red Fort similar to Agra, its name attributed to the red sandstone of its walls and adorned it with white marble pavilions including halls of public and private audience (Diwan-i-am where the Emperor would sit on a marbled paneled alcove, studded with gems, and hear complaints of the common people and Diwan-i-khas, where the Emperor held private meetings. This hall is made of marble, and its centre-piece used to be the Peacock Throne, which was studded with rubies and gems and was carried away to Iran by Nadir Shah in 1739. Domed and arched marble palaces, plush private apartments like Rang Mahal, the residence of the Emperor\u0092s wives and mistresses are also part of this magnificent complex. The Rang Mahal was crowned with gild turrets, skillfully painted and ornamented with a complex assortment of decorated with excellent paintings, gold bordered projections, mosaics of mirrors and the ceiling was made with gold and silverwhich wonderfully reflected in a central pool in the marble floor, a mosque (Moti Masjid) and richly designed gardens. Decorated with gold, precious stones and inlays of pietra dura work, the Red Fort still remains an impressive testimony to the Mughal splendor and might.",
          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/red-fort-1.webp",
          "historical_significance": "1700-1750 Medieval",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 28.656081,
          "lon": 77.2407959
        },
        {
          "id": "xrs5py29q",
          "title": "elhi \u0096 Monument -Jama Masjid",
          "name": "elhi \u0096 Monument -Jama Masjid",
          "desc": "Jama masjid, the biggest mosque in India started by Shah Jahan, is built of colossal proportions and has a courtyard, which can accommodate 25,000 devotees at a time. Constructed in sandstone and white marble, it combines the best of Hindu and Muslim styles of architecture, standing on 260 pillars supporting 15 domes at varying elevations. The vast courtyard is flanked by pillared corridors which run all along the courtyard and have domed pavilions in the middle on either side. The prayer hall has a magnificent facade of eleven arches, the central one being higher than the others and serving as the entrance. It is topped off by three magnificent domes which are richly ornamented and have black and white marble stripes.",
          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/jama-masjid-delhi.webp",
          "historical_significance": "",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 28.6506822,
          "lon": 77.232961
        },
        {
          "id": "phccay4ti",
          "title": "Delhi- Monument- Humayun\u0092s Tomb",
          "name": "Delhi- Monument- Humayun\u0092s Tomb",
          "desc": "1550-1600 Medieval Humayun\u0092s Tomb, built by his widow Haji Begum in 1564 is one of the most spectacular monument belonging to the Mughal period. A dazzling landmark in the evolution of Mughal architecture in India it is the first Indian building to use the Persian double dome and harmonious proportions. The red sandstone and marble structure is believed to be the inspiration for the designs of Taj Mahal. Standing on a stone platform, surrounded by a garden divided into quadrants by water channels. the plan of the building is simply brilliant and very mathematical. Enclosed by a high wall is a square garden divided initially into four large squares separated by causeways and channels, each square divided again into smaller squares by pathways (Charbagh) which is a typical Mughal garden. The intricate and delicately beautiful lattice work on the tomb remained the trademark of Mughal architecture. On the second storey, a high double dome of marble surmounts the roof with pillared chhatris placed around it.",
          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/humayun-tomb.webp",
          "historical_significance": "1550-1600 Medieval",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 28.227163134262387,
          "lon": 77.7125324904537
        },
        {
          "id": "zc1nc11sn",
          "title": "Delhi- Monument- Purana Qila",
          "name": "Delhi- Monument- Purana Qila",
          "desc": "1500-1550 Proto-historic The Purana Qila (Old Fort) stands on the site where the ancient city of Indraprashtha is believed to have existed. Archaeological excavations have yielded Painted Grey Ware pottery which has been dated to 1000BCE. Similar pottery has been noticed in other sites associated with the epic Mahabharata as well, which seems to prove that this indeed was the place where Indraprastha once flourished. The Old Fort was the citadel of the city of Dinapanah which Humayun started building in 1533 and completed five years later.",
          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/d_032.webp",
          "historical_significance": "1500-1550 Proto-historic",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 28.6091959,
          "lon": 77.2441111
        },
        {
          "id": "2yocsxdlb",
          "title": "Delhi- Monument- Safdarjang Tomb",
          "name": "Delhi- Monument- Safdarjang Tomb",
          "desc": "A tomb roughly on the plans of Humayun\u0092s tomb, though much smaller in scale is the Tomb of Safdarjung. The mausoleum built with red sandstone and buff stone is faced with marble and stands squarely in the middle of a garden which itself is on the pattern of the Mughal Charbagh style.",
          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/d_037.webp",
          "historical_significance": "1750-1800 Modern",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 28.5893249,
          "lon": 77.2125679
        },
        {
          "id": "xtr21nubn",
          "title": "Delhi- Monument -Lutyen\u0092s Delhi",
          "name": "Delhi- Monument -Lutyen\u0092s Delhi",
          "desc": "From the time the East India Company\u0092s first envoy stepped into India during Jahangir\u0092s rule in 1616 to the time they left in 1947 the British followed various architectural styles \u0096 Gothic, Imperial, Christian, English Renaissance and Victorian being the essentials. In 1911 the King Emperor George V passed an order declaring that the capital of British ruled India would be moved from Calcutta to Delhi New Delhi, was made by the British.",
          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/d_072.webp",
          "historical_significance": "1900-1947 Modern",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 28.6326238,
          "lon": 77.2070333
        },
        {
          "id": "hpidzk3qq",
          "title": "Delhi- Monument- Jantar Mantar",
          "name": "Delhi- Monument- Jantar Mantar",
          "desc": "1700-1750 Medieval The Jantar Mantar built in 1710 by Raja Jai Singh II of Jaipur in Delhi is an observatory consisting of mason-built astronomical instruments to chart the course of astronomical calculations. Jai Singh, who was a very scholarly king with a very keen interest in astronomy and astrology, had other observatories built in Ujjain, Jaipur, Mathura (which no longer survives) and Varanasi Since independence in 1947, New Delhi in particular has grown tremendously as a city and a number of significant architectural projects have been added to its landscape.",
          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/d_077.webp",
          "historical_significance": "1700-1750 Medieval",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 28.6271791,
          "lon": 77.2166166
        },
        {
          "id": "tm1bzvy92",
          "title": "Delhi- Monument- Birla Mandir",
          "name": "Delhi- Monument- Birla Mandir",
          "desc": "Birla Mandir is the first of the temples built across the country by the industrial family of Birla. Built in 1938, it was inaugurated by Mahatma Gandhi on the condition that people of all castes and especially untouchables would be allowed inside. The temple is designed in the Orissan style, with tall curved towers capped by large amalakas (circular ribbed motif at the summit of a temple tower). The exterior is faced with the white marble and red sandstone and the interior court is overlooked by two-storey verandahs on three sides and has a profusion of sculptures, idols, spires and jalis.",
          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/d_079.webp",
          "historical_significance": "1900-1947 Modern",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 28.6765917,
          "lon": 77.1987795
        },
        {
          "id": "rq75pcxr0",
          "title": "Lotus Temple",
          "name": "Lotus Temple",
          "desc": "The Baha'i House of Worship also known as the Lotus Temple is designed in the shape of a lotus. Its petals are constructed in concrete but are covered with white marble giving an extraordinary lightness to the structure. Nine pools of water around the structure create the illusion of a lotus floating in water. It belongs to the Baha'i Sect and is dedicated to the oneness of all religions.",
          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/BahaiLotusTemple.webp",
          "historical_significance": "1980-2000 Modern",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 28.5533586,
          "lon": 77.2586006
        },
        {
          "id": "paw0masrt",
          "title": "Delhi- Monument- Gyarah Murti",
          "name": "Delhi- Monument- Gyarah Murti",
          "desc": "The Gyarah Murti is a massive black sculpture in stone made by Devi Prasad Roy Choudhary, it depicts the famous Dandi March led by Mahatma Gandhi in 1931 against oppressive salt taxes imposed by the British regime. Delhi has a variety of museums dedicated to different subjects.",
          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/d_083.webp",
          "historical_significance": "1900-1947 Modern",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 28.6109034,
          "lon": 77.1907244
        },
        {
          "id": "pjbcpf42m",
          "title": "Archaeological Museum, RedFort",
          "name": "Archaeological Museum, RedFort",
          "desc": "Situated in the historic Red Fort, the Archaeological Museum exhibits objects that date to the Mughal period. On view are manuscripts and firmans that display the fine art of calligraphy. Also on view are paintings, textiles and costumes. The museum also houses relics of the 1857 revolt like maps and weapons.",
          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/d_088.webp",
          "historical_significance": "1850-1900 Modern",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 29.081905398947665,
          "lon": 77.84100892859152
        },
