      "fairs_and_festivals": [
        {
          "id": "140ie251e",
          "title": "Delhi-Fairs & Festivals- Phoolwalon Ki Sair",
          "name": "Delhi-Fairs & Festivals- Phoolwalon Ki Sair",
          "desc": "Phoolwalon Ki Sair (walk of the florists) is a festival that has continued from the time of the Mughals, who encouraged the celebrations as a means of bonding among people. This annual festival of flowersellers, is a festival in which both Hindus and Muslims participate. Phoolwalon-ki-Sair or the Sair-e-Gulafroshan, a three-day festival celebrated simultaneously at the dargah of Khwaja Bakhtiyar Kaki and at the ancient shrine of Devi Jog Maya in Mehrauli. Kathak dances, qawwalis, a blaze of lights, huge pankhas (fans) made of palm leaves, decorated with tinsel and flowers and acrobats delight a vast audience. A huge procession, led by fire dancers, takes the flower pankhas through the streets of Mehrauli. Audio/video of Qawali",
          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/phoolwalon-ki-sair.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
          "materials": ""
        },
        {
          "id": "3awl17op0",
          "title": "Delhi- Fairs & Festivals-Dilli Haat",
          "name": "Delhi- Fairs & Festivals-Dilli Haat",
          "desc": "Situated in the heart of the city, the Dilli Haat is a version of the traditional weekly rural market, offering a delightful amalgam of craft, food and other cultural activities. However, while the village haat is a mobile, flexible arrangement at Dilli Haat, a permanent haat, it is the craftsmen who are mobile and ever-changing thereby offering a kaleidoscope of the richness and diversity of Indian handicrafts and artifacts. A wide variety of skilfully crafted handicrafts, intrinsic to each part of the country are seen in this exotic bazaar. These range from Delhi has seen the rise and fall of many empires which have left behind a plethora of monuments that tell tales of the grandeur and glory of bygone ages. A city which traces its history to Mahabharata, the great epic tale of wars fought between the Kauravas and the Pandavas for the city of Indraprastha. If some monuments remind the rule of the mighty Mughals and Lodhis, others speak of the rich legacy that the British had left behind. In the late tenth century, Lalkot was the provincial Rajput centre ruled by Prithviraj Chauhan who was defeated by the Ghurids of Ghazni under the command of Qutab-ub-din Aibak. Mehrauli on the outskirts of the southern part of Delhi is known for the Qutab complex of monuments.",
          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/dillihaat.webp",
          "historical_significance": "1980-2000 Contemporary",
          "materials": ""
        },
        {
          "id": "3dc2a361",
          "title": "Phoolwalon Ki Sair",
          "name": "Phoolwalon Ki Sair",
          "desc": "Phoolwalon Ki Sair",
          "img": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fd/Panka_%28fan%29_over_the_Idol_of_Yogmaya_in_the_sanctum.JPG"
        },
        {
          "id": "aa3ed715",
          "title": "Dilli Haat",
          "name": "Dilli Haat",
          "desc": "Dilli Haat",
          "img": "https://dynamic-media-cdn.tripadvisor.com/media/photo-o/07/2b/60/79/dilli-haat.jpg?w=900&h=500&s=1"
        }
      ],
      "museums": [
        {
          "id": "3fzjmvs7k",
          "title": "Delhi- Museum-Teen Murti Bhawan",
          "name": "Delhi- Museum-Teen Murti Bhawan",
          "desc": "Teen Murti Bhawan was the home of Jawaharlal Nehru, India\u0092s first Prime Minister. Formerly called Flagstaff House, it used to be the house of the British Commander-in-Chief. This was the residence of Nehru from 1948 till his death in 1964. Having a fac\u0327ade of stone and stucco in an austere classical style, it is preserved as a museum and a library of modern Indian history in memory of Jawaharlal Nehru.",
          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/d_085a.webp",
          "historical_significance": "1900-1947 Modern",
          "materials": ""
        },
        {
          "id": "pcpx6y40q",
          "title": "Sanskriti Museum",
          "name": "Sanskriti Museum",
          "desc": "Located on the outskirts of Delhi, the Sanskriti Museum is a treasure house of three museums - Sanskriti Museum of Indian Terracotta, museum of textiles and Sanskriti Museum of Everyday Art. Terracotta displays earthen objects made by some of the finest crafts persons, hailing from different parts of India, using their distinctive traditions and techniques. This complex also has crafts persons, where one can see them at work. It exhibits over 150 terracotta items collected from all over the country. The Sanskriti Museum of Everyday Art was set up to preserve the age-old traditions of India. It has functional items of everyday use, like jars, combs, mirrors, toys and kitchen utensils, on its display.",
          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/Sanskriti_Museum.webp",
          "historical_significance": "1980-2000 Contemporary",
          "materials": ""
        },
        {
          "id": "74g6as2dr",
          "title": "National Museum",
          "name": "National Museum",
          "desc": "The National Museum offers a unique insight into the history and culture of India. The museum offers splendid and priceless pieces of Indian art and sculpture that have been arranged in galleries, according to the period to which they belong. It displays a fine collection of fine bronze idols,stone sculptures, textiles, coins and tribal art. The famous sculpture of the Dancing Girl from Mohenjodaro belonging to 2,500 BCE is one of the prized possessions here. A collection of Indian miniature paintings from Mughal, Rajasthani and Pahari schools are also part of the display.",
          "img": "/state-images/Delhi/national-museum.webp",
          "historical_significance": "1947-2000",
          "materials": ""
        },
        {
