        "handicrafts": [
          {
            "id": "6ued7rzx8",
            "title": "Pottery and Terracota",
            "name": "Pottery and Terracota",
            "desc": "Goa is rich in pottery which is deep rich red in colour, with a smooth surface, and has a charm and style of its own. A large variety of domestic ware which includes attractive water and flower pots, a wide range of figures and panels are also made including figures of saints and Gods and Godesses and animals, ashtrays and pen holders,etc. These depict an old men or women, mother with a child or two children playing, and a variety of such themes. Borde and Bicholim are two famous centres of earthenware in Goa where both utility cum decorative items are produced by the potters.",
            "img": "/state-images/Goa/G_032.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "05yb7m2hr",
            "title": "Woodwork",
            "name": "Woodwork",
            "desc": "Goa has an old and rich tradition of wood carving. After the coming of the Portuguese, this craft was turned largely to a magnificent quality in furniture made in western style. However, oriental designs are used in purely floral, animal and human figures. Main places of production are Verem, Bardez and Cuncolim.",
            "img": "/state-images/Goa/G_033.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "u3zx3a8qx",
            "title": "Brass and Metal Craft",
            "name": "Brass and Metal Craft",
            "desc": "Goa is famous for its brass items, especially samais. It is a custom in Goa that at the time of marriage the parents of the bride present her a set of metalware like kalsa, kalshi, puncharati, ekarati etc. Brass metal casting is a craft passed on from one generation to another. The items produced",
            "img": "/state-images/Goa/G_033a.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "ojmavco3j",
            "title": "Bamboo Craft",
            "name": "Bamboo Craft",
            "desc": "Bamboo craft is one of the traditional crafts of Goa and practiced in almost all parts of the state. Bambaoo craft in Goa was practiced here by the mahars, a community of Goa. Initially they produced utility items required by farmers and fishermen for drying and storing of food grains and selling fish, etc. The range of items produced during that time and still in use in villages include patlo (baskets), valli (used for washing rice and grains), supli (for cleaning rice), mats of different sizes (used also as shades for windows for protection against rain and sunlight) and dali (one of the most important item for farmers for drying food grains; it can also be used as floor coverings).",
            "img": "/state-images/Goa/G_035.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "2c1md0anm",
            "title": "Fibre Craft",
            "name": "Fibre Craft",
            "desc": "The craft of sisal fibre craft was brought to Goa by nuns from Kerala. Full of ornamentation and decoration, these are bascially utilitarian objects. Attractive and colourful shopping bags, ladies purses, coasters and wall hangings are made from banana or sisal fiber.",
            "img": "/state-images/Goa/G_036b.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
