      "museums": [
        {
          "id": "00q0ekkcw",
          "title": "Goa- Museum- Archaeological Museum and Portrait Gallery",
          "name": "Goa- Museum- Archaeological Museum and Portrait Gallery",
          "desc": "Archaeological Museum and Portrait Gallery is at the Convent of St. Francis of Assisi, old Goa. It has the collection of sculptures covering the period before the arrival of the Portuguese. The main gallery has Hindu statues such as Uma- Mahesh. The portraits of Vasco da Gama and Portuguese Governors are placed on the first floor which provides an interesting study in the evolution of court dresses .The museum also boasts of a large coin collection that traces back the history of Goa. One of the main occupations of the early Portuguese in Goa was building of churches and in fact one of Vasco da Gama's main missions for finding the sea route to India was to seek Christians and spices. Christianity was forced upon by the Portuguese during the period of the Inquisition with wide scale destruction of temples and this continued till the official end of the Inquisition in Goa in 1812. In fact, the first Hindu temple allowed to be constructed by the Portuguese in 300 years was in 1818 at Panaji. With a significant population of Goans being Christians for many generations today, the Church is an important factor in Goa's socio-cultural and religious life. The architecture of Goa's churches have undergone notable changes with the passage of time and the fashion of the era that they were built in.",
          "img": "",
          "historical_significance": "Modern 1947-1980",
          "materials": ""
        }
      ],
