    {
      "id": "gujarat",
      "name": "Gujarat",
      "geography": {
        "capital": "Gandhinagar",
        "key_cities": []
      },
      "facts": [
        "Step-wells are one of India's most unique, but little-known, contributions to architecture. The concept of the vav, or stepwell, was a response to the harsh climate of Gujarat and Rajasthan and the acute shortage of water. The practice of making wells into an art form was begun by the Hindus of the Solanki kings but it developed under the Muslim rule. The galleries and chambers surrounding these wells were often carved profusely with elaborate detail and became cool, quiet retreats during the hot summers.",
        "**Rani ki Vav- Patan**: The Rani Vav at Patan, was built during the late eleventh century, by the Queen Udaymati after the death of her husband, King Bhimdeva I of the Solanki dynasty. A massive structure of seven storeys, it is probably the most magnificent step-well in Gujarat. Multi-storey colonnades and retaining walls link a stepped tank to a deep circular well. Throughout, there is a rich ornamentation. Columns, brackets and beams are encrusted with scrollwork and the wall niches are carved with figures. The designs on walls and pillars include, leaves, flowers, birds, fish and other breathtaking ornamental designs. At water level the figure of Vishnu on Seshanaga and the Buddha is included among the Dasavataras, the ten incarnations of Lord Vishnu.",
        "**Embroidery**: is an integral part of life in Gujarat from superbly embroidered toranas to chaklas or wall hangings, beautifully embroidered dresses like lehngas, cholis and dupattas, men\u0092s kurtas, churidhars, childrens clothes and so on. Aribharat or hook embroidery, mochibarat, rabari, banni and soof embroidery and applique work are among the many beautiful embroideries of this state. Abhala is the embroidery where small round pieces of mirrors are fixed on to the fabric using buttonhole stitching; the embroidery is done in a herringbone stitch using silken thread. Rust, light green, indigo, blue, deep red, pink, and purple are the colours used. Kathi is the oldest embroidery which is known for its romantic motifs. Geometrical motifs are fabricated with multicolored fabric pieces leading to patch work effect. Varieties of items are prepared. Heer is an embossed stitch having shades of off-white, yellow, madder red, black, indigo, ivory, and green. Small mirror pieces are used to add more beauty to the embroidery. Ari embroidery with silk threads using a hook is a popular craft of Kutch. The motifs found commonly are, dancing peacocks, human figures in dancing poses etc. A Bandhani pattern with beautiful and delicate bead work is unique. The various communities in Gujarat, rabaris, ahirs, jats, bharwads have their own styles of embroidery. The embroidery of the Rabari community is usually done on a maroon background with the enclosed motifs. A style of embroidery in Saurashtra known as mahajan has a special design called kajuri in which a satin shine and fine effects are attained by special manipulation of the satin and herringbone stitches with interplay of glowing red and shades of violet. Applique is an integral part of the decorative needlework of Gujarat with its own distinctive style. It is done on patchwork in which pieces of coloured and patterned fabrics are finely cut in different shapes and sizes and then sewn together on a plain background to form a composite piece. Saurashtra and Kutch are also noted for their bead work whose designs lean heavily on the embroidery and the bandhini patterns. A whole gamut of articles are made of beads like decorations for domestic animals, cradles, coverlets, hangings, toranas, purses, little perches that sit on a woman's head to balance water pots, fans and a whole range of ornamental pieces for the house."
      ],
      "art_forms": {
        "paintings": [
          {
            "id": "jqrou47nq",
            "title": "The basic and foremost feature of this unique form of painting is an exhaustive detailing of each and every component relating to the scene.",
            "name": "The basic and foremost feature of this unique form of painting is an exhaustive detailing of each and every component relating to the scene.",
            "desc": "There are four primary schools of Indian Miniature Paintings - the Rajput, the Mughal, Pahari and the Deccan schools. Miniature Painters used varied substances for coloring their drawings, namely- minerals, vegetables, precious stones, indigo, conch shells, pure gold and silver. The illustrated manuscripts of Jains and Buddhists, and the flowering of the Mughal, Rajput, Pahari and Deccan Miniatures are noted for their meticulous execution and artistic skills. The fine stroke of brushes conveyed the themes from the Ramayana, Mahabharata, Bhagvata Purana, Rasikpriya, Rasamanjiri, etc. The themes have also emerged from the compositions of the great poets like Sur, Keshav, Bihari, Matiram, Dev and Puhukar who wrote great poems like Sursagar, Bihari Satsai, Rasraj, Ashtayam and Rasbeli. There are several themes of different nature. They centered on the love of Radha and Krishna. definite narrative content. These brilliantly illustrated paintings glow as if illuminated from inside. The use of bold contrasts such as red, green, blue yellow is definitely a native influence. Artists belonging to Pahari school of painting used bright colors and depicted themes of love. These Paintings from the Hills, were developed at the Hindu royal courts of Kulu, Guler, Mankot, Nurpur, Mandi, Kangra and Basoholi regions of the North Indian mountain valleys.",
            "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/miniature-painting-1.webp",
            "historical_significance": "",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "aoeic577i",
            "title": "Raja Ravi Verma & his paintings",
            "name": "Raja Ravi Verma & his paintings",
            "desc": "Raja Ravi Varma was born on April 29, 1848 at Kilimanoor, a small town in Kerala. His uncle was a well known artist known as Raja Raja Varma who gave him elementary art lessons. He was taken to Thiruvananthapuram in his fourteenth year to stay in the royal palace and learn oil painting. Ravi Varma had many opportunities to discover and learn new techniques and media in the field of painting. His later years spent in Mysore, Baroda and other parts of the country enabled him to sharpen and expand his skills and blossom into a mature and complete painter. He was the first artist to cast the Indian Gods and mythological characters in natural earthy surroundings using European realism. His dazzling oil paintings of India\u0092s ancient glory and his mass reproductions through oleography reached out to the Indian populace in an extraordinary scale at the turn of the twentieth Century. Varma had already made a name for himself as a highly talented painter who had mastered the European painter, the Odore Jensen at the Travancore court. His technique of oil painting observing a visiting Dutch paintings had won prizes at Madras and had been highly appreciated by the British governor too. But Baroda, definitely, was a major turning point in his career. Varma was invited to paint a portrait of the 18-year-old Sayajirao Gaekwad in 1881. The young prince was so happy with the result that he not only commissioned Varma to paint portraits of many of his family members thereafter, but also got him to execute more artistically ambitious works on themes drawn from Puranic legends, Sanskrit classics and mythological epics to decorate the newly built Lakshmi Vilas palace. Varma, was one of the first Indian artist to adopt the style of French and British naturalist painters by adopting devices like the chiaroscuro and dramatic lighting effects in the oil-on-canvas medium. But Varma employed these western techniques to paint purely Indian themes. Episodes from literary and mythological sources like those of Dushyant-Shakuntala, Nala-Damayanti, Radha-Krishna, Harishchandra-Taramati, the Mahabharata and the Ramayana were his favourite subjects.",
            "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/rajaravivarma.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Modern 1800-1850",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "72175147",
            "title": "Miniature & folk painting",
            "name": "Miniature & folk painting",
