      "museums": [
        {
          "id": "6mg6zy82c",
          "title": "Gujarat- Museum- Archaeological Museum, Lothal",
          "name": "Gujarat- Museum- Archaeological Museum, Lothal",
          "desc": "The Archaeological museum at Lothal was set up in 1976 to display the artifacts that were recovered from the excavations conducted from 1952 to 1961 at this site. The museum has three galleries which are treasure houses for beads, terracotta ornaments, replicas of seals, shell and ivory objects, copper and bronze objects, mirrors of bronze and copper, perforated and painted potteries, toy bullock carts and a variety of objects made from stone, shell, and bone and tools and potteries yielded from excavations. The most fabulous are, steatite seals with unicorn motifs and inscription, terracotta seals with elephant motifs, seal, gold necklaces, copper and bronze fish hooks, bangles, perforated jars, painted jars, terracotta bull, horse, model boat, ivory scales and shell compass, chess dices, etc. There are maps and drawings depicting an artist\u0092s impression of the Harappan town of Lothal. One of the galleries showcases game objects, animal and human figurines, weights, painted potteries, objects recovered from burials and ritual objects, miniature potteries, bricks besides a replica of a joint burial and a scaled plaster of Paris model of the city based on reconstructions made by archeologists.",
          "img": "https://parametric-architecture.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Indias-National-Maritime-Heritage-Complex-The-Worlds-Largest-Maritime-Museum-Rising-in-Gujarat-03-1600x900.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Modern 1947-1980",
          "materials": ""
        },
        {
          "id": "nkifrr3fi",
          "title": "Calico Museum collection",
          "name": "Calico Museum collection",
          "desc": "One of the finest textile museums in the world it is housed in a carved wooden haveli. Located in the Sarabhai Foundation, in Shahibagh the Calico Museum of Textiles, was constructed in 1949. Along with the finest collection of not just textiles and clothes the museum boasts of furniture, temple artifacts and crafts of the country. It has a collection of the finest fabrics spun, woven, printed and painted in different parts of India during the last five centuries. One can see the exquisite designs and artwork in kalamkaris, pichhwais, patolas, bandhnis, floor spreads, tent canopies and precious brocades.It has a collection of marble, sandstone and bronze icons and busts split in two thematic sections- gallery for religious textiles and historical textiles. There are colourful embroidered wall hangings depicting legends of Krishna, cloth decorated with tie and dye, glinting mirror work, screen prints, block prints and intricate embroidery. There is an embroidered tent and the robes of Shah Jahan, along with elaborate carpets and plump cushions that once furnished Muslim palace. Special exhibits include Kashmiri shawls, Kullu embroidery, glittering silk brocades from Varanasi, folk art from the Punjab and masks and large wooden temple rathas from Tamil Nadu. The collection also includes some of the best examples of the Patola saris woven in Patan as well as the extravagant Zari work that gilds saris in heavy gold stitching which weigh almost nine kilos.",
          "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/calico-2.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Modern 1947-1980",
          "materials": ""
        },
        {
          "id": "3b19mo6qb",
          "title": "Vechaar Utensils Museum",
          "name": "Vechaar Utensils Museum",
          "desc": "is a unique museum located on the outskirts of Ahmedabad city. Established in 1981, this museum exhibits utensils of all shapes and sizes. The exhibits have been collected from all over the country and have been displayed around a simple courtyard. The collection includes pots used for storing water cooking and serving utensils, spoons, rolling pins, tiffin carriers, vessel for churning buttermilk, storage jars, feeding cups for infants and havan kunds too. The collection includes pottery, terracotta and wooden articles as well. The display gives a glimpse into the lifestyle of people of the place they belong to and also the craftsmanship involved.",
          "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/vechaar-1.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
          "materials": ""
        },
        {
          "id": "dtpjoi18n",
          "title": "Kite Museum",
          "name": "Kite Museum",
          "desc": "Museum situated in Sanskar Kendra the museum was established in 1985 having a fabulous collection of kites from all over the world. There are artistic kites, rare kites that have been crafted from more than 400 pieces of paper. It is the second such museum in the world. Bhanubhai Shah, a conoisseur of kites is the brain behind the concept of the museum and he is responsible for designing and donating kites to the museum. garba dances, mirror work and block prints,the kites are of different styles. The kites are made from different materials as well. The materials include paper, cloth, polythene, and nylon. One of the most lavishly designed and decorated kites are the hexagonal Rokoku - beautiful Japanese kites.",
          "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/kites-1.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Contemporary 1980-",
          "materials": ""
        },
        {
          "id": "4691b2wup",
          "title": "Gujarat- Museum- Shreyas Folk Museum \u0096 Ahemadabad",
          "name": "Gujarat- Museum- Shreyas Folk Museum \u0096 Ahemadabad",
          "desc": "A really informative and educational museum located close to the suburbs of Ambavadi near Sabarmati is the Shreyas Folk Museum. This museum displays the colorful and traditional folk arts of Gujarat. From textile and clothing to the decorative crafts, the Shreyas Folk Museum brings out the traditional rustic flavor and cultural heritage of Gujarat. The Museum has an exclusive collection of folk arts and objects which reflect the colorful and traditional heritage of Gujarat and gives an insight into the traditional crafts and technology. The collections reflect many of the traditional and rural communities. These communities belong to different villages of Gujarat and in this museum one can get a glimpse of their exquisite artwork. The communities whose culture and folk art are being researched here are Kathi, Rabari, Rharan, Ahir, Brahmins, Banias, Meghvals, Bharvad, Kanbi, Koli, Mer, Bhansali, Rajputs, Khojas, Vohras, Memons and the Miyanas. One of the unique attractions is a complete skeleton of an elephant which has a height of 3.19m. There is an extensive collection of old coins, weapons, puppets, toys, costumes, masks, musical fountains and animal caparisons which recall the grandeur of the bygone era and projects a vintage charm.",
          "img": "https://dynamic-media-cdn.tripadvisor.com/media/photo-o/16/41/ee/dd/best-capture-of-museum.jpg?w=1200&h=-1&s=1",
          "historical_significance": "Modern 1947-1980",
          "materials": ""
        },
        {
          "id": "zlyfkobl1",
          "title": "Gujarat- Museum- The NC Mehta Gallery \u0096 Ahmedabad",
          "name": "Gujarat- Museum- The NC Mehta Gallery \u0096 Ahmedabad",
          "desc": "Designed by the famous architect Le Corbusier, the NC Mehta Gallery is one of the museums in Ahmedabad which houses an exclusive range of miniature paintings collected from various parts of India, these miniature paintings are the collections of Late Shri Nanalal C. Mehta who was one of the pioneers of Indian miniature painting and the NC Mehta Gallery, Ahmedabad was named after him. He published several books on Sanskrit and Hindi literature and discovered new documents on Indian paintings. Vasanta Vilasa scroll and Chaurapanchasika are some of the treasured collections apart from miniature paintings that include sacred works, ragmala paintings, royal portraits and different social scenes. The Gallery has examples of Western India and Sultanate School paintings, folios of the Jain Kalpasutra and also the Pre-Mughal style folios. One can see folios of the Hamza-Nama and the Sikander Nama which are two of the famous Persian epics. These miniature paintings were mostly drawn on handmade paper with mineral colors. The aim of the display is to inculcate the love for art and paintings in our society and also for the research and further studies in art work.",
          "img": "https://dynamic-media-cdn.tripadvisor.com/media/photo-o/0a/03/e8/4e/nc-mehta-gallery.jpg?w=1200&h=-1&s=1",
          "historical_significance": "Modern 1947-1980",
          "materials": ""
        },
        {
          "id": "44k58koj5",
          "title": "Gujarat- Museum- Junagarh",
          "name": "Gujarat- Museum- Junagarh",
          "desc": "An ancient fortified city rich in myth and legend, Junagadh has beautiful shrines of Hindus, Jains and Muslims. Deriving its name from the 'old fort', which circles this medieval town, Junagadh was the capital of the Kshattrapa dynasty. The Girnar Hills that landscape Junagadh are a holy refuge to the Jains. The history of Mount Girnar dates back to the empire of Ashoka, who inscribed 14 edicts on a huge boulder en-route to Mt. Girnar peak. There is a huge boulder, housed in a small roadside building, on which are fourteen rock edicts inscribed by Emperor Ashoka.",
          "img": "/state-images/Gujarat/Girnar-hill.webp",
          "historical_significance": "",
          "materials": ""
        },
        {
          "id": "8pmjbk5kx",
          "title": "Vadodara- Museum- Maharaja Fateh Singh Museum",
          "name": "Vadodara- Museum- Maharaja Fateh Singh Museum",
          "desc": "Maharaja Fateh Singh Museum is a museum situated in a corner of the 750-acre grounds of the Lakshmi Vilas Palace built by Sayajirao III, the highly progressive ruler of the former princely state of Baroda. This used to be the royal school for the children of the family. The works of art belonging to the Royal family are displayed in the museum. The major works of art in this museum are the paintings by European and Indian artists including a collection of the commissioned paintings by Raja Ravi Varma. The collection includes portraits of the Royal family in addition to the paintings based on Hindu mythology for which Raja Ravi Varma was famous. There is a collection of sculptures in marble and bronze. These include copies of great masters in bronze commissioned by the royal family of Baroda. This includes original works of renowned artists like Fellicci, an Italian artist whose works adorn not only the Museum but also the Lakshmi Vilas Palace. Some of Fellici's works can be seen in the Public Park known as Sayaji Garden. The museum has an oriental gallery which houses Japanese and Chinese sculptures, Korean ceramics, Japanese screens, copies of Renaissance masterpieces etc. These works were mainly collected by Maharaja Sayajirao Gaekwad during his visits abroad.",
          "img": "https://www.tourmyindia.com/images/maharaja-fateh-singh-museum1.jpg",
          "historical_significance": "Modern 1850-1900",
          "materials": ""
        },
        {
