    {
      "id": "himachal-pradesh",
      "name": "Himachal Pradesh",
      "geography": {
        "capital": "Shimla",
        "key_cities": []
      },
      "facts": [
        "**Himachal Pradesh- Chamba**: Chamba stands on a plateau on the bank of Ravi. It blossomed as the ancient capital of the Pahari Kings and was named after King Sahil Varman\u0092s daughter Champavati who is considered the patron deity of the place.",
        "For centuries, wood has been used in Himachal in the construction and ornamentation of temples, homes, idols and so on. The skill is passed on from one generation to the other. The master wood carver of Chamba, Malik Lateef, for example, belongs to a family of traditional carpenters. His father Ali Baksh worked in the courts of the Chamba king and his artifacts are still preserved in the Bhuri Singh Museum in Chamba.",
        "**Jewellery**: in Himachal is artistic, elaborate in style and is inspired from nature itself. The designs reflect nature in simple motifs like seeds, flowers and leaves being developed into exquisite patterns. Different communities wear jewellery unique to their traditions, but some ornaments are worn all over the state, which include the hemispherical shaped chak \u0096 worn on the forehead and both sides of the head, neck ornaments like hansli or small pendants called toke, chandanhaar - a necklace comprising five or seven rows of facetted gold beads. The choker worn here is called kach and consists of silver beads and triangular plaques. Chiri tikka is a flat piece of silver, enamelled or embedded with pearls and suspended from the centre of the forehead while several chains hang along the hairline on both sides. A heavy bunch of silver tassels or flowers, attached to the plait with a smaller ornament is tied to the plait ends. Men, women and children often wear silver amulets to ward off evil spirits. Sunarion ki Gaon near Rohru is a village, where many families are engaged in jewellery designing and crafting.",
        "Historically, the craft mainly flourished during the reign of Raja Umed Singh of Chamba (1748-68). The oldest rumal dates back to the 16th century and is said to have been embroidered by Bebe Nanki, sister of Guru Nanak. This piece is now kept in the Sikh shrine in Hoshiarpur. Another important rumal was presented by Raja Gopal Singh to the British in 1883 and is now displayed at the Victoria Albert Museum, London. This piece depicts the battle of Kurukshetra.",
        "The Paharis are partial to a whole lot of musical instruments without which pahari music wouldn\u0092t be half as interesting. The kangarange is the most commonly used stringed instrument. Among the wind instruments are the bhopal, shamal, bugial, and shehnai (different kinds of pipes), the damentu (horn), the highly ornate narsingha (hornpipe) and the sanai (hautboy). To keep the rhythm of the various dances percussion instruments like the damane, anga, dhol and dholak (drums), the hulki (an hourglass-shaped drum), the kartal (clappers), khanjiri (tambourine) and jhanja (large cymbals) are played."
      ],
