        "handicrafts": [
          {
            "id": "vxsljh82l",
            "title": "Himachal Pradesh- Handicrafts",
            "name": "Himachal Pradesh- Handicrafts",
            "desc": "The arts and crafts of any region are a reflection of its environment, people and traditions. So it is in Himachal. Weaving, carving, painting, or chiselling \u0096 are an intrinsic part of the Himachal life. From the upper reaches of Lahaul and Spiti down to the lowlands of Kangra \u0096 life and its shades are woven in, painted on, felt in soulful rhythms or celebrated with joyous abandon, carved in, engraved whether it is in the miniature paintings of Kangra, the thangka of Spiti, or the beautiful shawls of Kullu.",
            "img": "http://hptdc.in/wp-content/uploads/2019/06/chamba_rumaal.jpg",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "vts9jtyys",
            "title": "Himachal Pradesh- Handicrafts- Wood",
            "name": "Himachal Pradesh- Handicrafts- Wood",
            "desc": "Availability of wood abundantly in its forests makes it used profusely for architecture in Himachal Pradesh. The pine and deodar, besides walnut, horse chestnut and wild black mulberry are used for woodcraft. Wood carvings on domestic items are quite different from those in temples or houses. Prominent amongst these designs are: nagbel\u0097a zigzag line that combines the serpentine pattern with three or four petal flowers in each side of the line; dori\u0097a continuous line of flowers; kutheri phool\u0097a multi-sided figure, sometimes enlarged and the pattern lid in a jali to serve as a ventilator.Houses in all over the valleys of Himachal are extensively ornamented with carvings \u0096 on doors, windows, balcony panels etc. The craft also translates into wooden idols of gods and goddesses in traditional styles. Utilitarian objects crafted in wood can be often found in Pahari homes \u0096 these may include rectangular boxes to store grains or ornaments. The Gaddi households in Chamba and Brahmaur are famous for their attractive wooden utensils. Villages of Kinnaur still use wooden household utensils extensively. Intricately carved wooden spinning wheels are also in use.",
            "img": "/state-images/Himachal_Pradesh/wooden-devi_mask.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "qlnmufb34",
            "title": "Himachal Pradesh- Handicrafts- Wool",
            "name": "Himachal Pradesh- Handicrafts- Wool",
            "desc": "It is the extreme cold climate of Himachal that necessitated wool weaving. Almost every household in Himachal owns a pit-loom and it is not unusual to find men and women spinning yarn on spindle The best-known woven object is the shawl, ranging from extremely fine pashmina to the coarse desar. Kullu in particular has been famous for its shawls with striking geometrical patterns and vibrant colours \u0096 the distinctive feature being stripes running along the edges. Caps of Himachal are of distinctive styles and are different in every part of the state. In Kinnaur, not only shawls, but also saris, trousers and pyjamas are woven in wool. The shawls woven in Rampur, known as Rampur chaddar, are famous for their soft texture and durability. In Chamba district, the weaving assumes a chequered pattern. Besides shawls, carpets and blankets are an essential part of the Himachal lifestyle. Carpets in brilliant colours are woven with a variety of traditional motifs \u0096 there are garudas on flowering trees, dragons, swastikas, flutes symbolising happiness or lotus blooms signifying purity. Carpets are woven as furnishing, as saddles for horses and as blankets or chutkas. They are also a part of every bride\u0091s trousseau.",
            "img": "/state-images/Himachal_Pradesh/Himachal-wool.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "4zz6u8tfj",
            "title": "Himachal Pradesh- Handicrafts- Stone",
            "name": "Himachal Pradesh- Handicrafts- Stone",
            "desc": "The Shivalik hills have large resources of fine sandstone, which is highly suited for carving, and has played a vital role in keeping alive the stone carvers\u0092 craft. Numerous stone temples still dot the Himachal landscape. Kangra, Mandi, Bilaspur, Sirmaur, Chamba and Kullu have been traditional centres for stone carving. The giant monolithic carving of the rock cut temple at Masrur in Kangra has been compared to the Mahabalipuram temples for its daring and detailed, elaborate and delicate chiselling.",
            "img": "https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/1194/1498/files/Screenshot_2024-07-11_at_11.35.52_AM_480x480.png?v=1720677972",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
