        {
          "id": "fg1owah5z",
          "title": "Kangra Fort",
          "name": "Kangra Fort",
          "desc": "is located in the valley at the confluence of the Banganga and Manjhi rivulets which serve as a natural moat for the fort. Having seven gateways in the fort, Ranjit Singh Darwaza is one with a massive wooden door flanked by fortifications on both sides. Just before the Andheri Darwaza, a path ascends westwards to the wooded plateau where an isolated mehrab (arch) of the non- existent mosque from Jahangir\u0092s days is hidden under thick foliage. Nearby, to the south of the mehrab, is a large stone stepwell, called Kapoorsagar, and other structures in ruins. But the most interesting part of the fort is inside the Darshani Darwaza. This gate is one of",
          "img": "/state-images/Himachal_Pradesh/kangra-fort-1.webp",
          "historical_significance": "1000-1100",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 32.0867046,
          "lon": 76.2533449
        },
        {
          "id": "950ck25dy",
          "title": "Himachal Pradesh- Monument- Kye Gompa",
          "name": "Himachal Pradesh- Monument- Kye Gompa",
          "desc": "Kye Gompa built in the fourteenth century, the largest and oldest repository of Buddhist learning in the Western Himalayas in the Spiti region has the appearance of a hill fort. In summer very few of the Lamas are in residence, the majority living in their own villages till winter when they return within the sacred walls. Some 200 or 300 monks reside in this monastery in winter. The Gompa has a narrow passage and a winding flight of steps that leads to the topmost building around an open courtyard. There at the top on a raised platform is the zimchung, the chamber where the incarnate of the lama resides. The gompa is a three storeyed structure one of which is literally a basement.The basement is used as the store which stores the treasures of the monastery \u0096 old weapons and other artifacts. The gompa has a valuable collection of ancient Buddhist texts called Kangyur. They are also kept in the basement area in a separate square vault with walls having beautiful religious murals. The ground floor of the gompa has an oblong room used as the du khang. The walls of this room are also highly painted with beautiful murals with themes from the jataka and other popular stories. To the right side of the du khang is the gonkhang, a refectory and other rooms. The cells of the monks are box\u0096like chambers all around at the lower levels. The gompa is also well known for its priceless collection of ancient thangkas.",
          "img": "/state-images/Himachal_Pradesh/kye-gompa.webp",
          "historical_significance": "1300-1400",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 31.950479577210544,
          "lon": 77.73522549979063
        },
        {
          "id": "1lb7ej0sh",
          "title": "Himachal Pradesh- Monument- Tabo Monastery",
          "name": "Himachal Pradesh- Monument- Tabo Monastery",
          "desc": "Tabo Monastery is the oldest Buddhist Monastery in Spiti Valley. It is also one of the most important Gompa in the entire Tibetan Buddhist world. This is the place where the present Dalai Lama will retire to from his duties. High mountains surround the gompa. It was found by Rinchen Tsangpo in 996 CE.. He brought in Kashmiri artists to decorate this gompa which is second in merit only to the Tholing Gompa in Tibet. It is popularly known as the Gem of the Himalayas and has a preserve of ancient Buddhist legacy and houses some of the most sacred texts of Tibetan Buddhism. It also houses a priceless collection of manuscripts and thangkas. Among its treasures are historical murals depicting tales from the Buddhist pantheon. The old section has 9 temples, 23 chortens, a chamber for monks and another for nuns. Five halls arranged in a row are enclosed within the gompa walls. The colourful murals of Tabo come very close to the pure Indian style identified with the rock\u0096cut Ajanta and Ellora caves. The technique required the surface to be coated with several thin layers of lime and yak skin glue. It was then burnished vigorously to provide the ground. Next it was smoothed and freshened with animal fat and butter. Natural vegetable dyes and powdered stone colours were mixed with dzo milk and yak urine for painting. The early Indian style murals used a combination of reds and yellows with little stress on landscaping. The area around the principal figures was filled with smaller divinities. In contrast, the images of Tabo wear a serene smile and half\u0096shut dreamy eyes showing introspective devotion.",
          "img": "/state-images/Himachal_Pradesh/tabo-monastery.webp",
          "historical_significance": "900-1000 CE",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 31.217539142344915,
          "lon": 77.59012863295911
        },
        {
          "id": "uyejpf532",
          "title": "Wooden Chhattries of Lakshminarayan Temple",
          "name": "Wooden Chhattries of Lakshminarayan Temple",
          "desc": "Chamba has numerous ancient temples patterned in the style of local hill architecture made from both wood and stone. Most of the temples in the region including Lakshminarayan, Hari Rai Temple, Champavati temple, Vajreshwari Temple, Hari Har Temple etc. were built in the period between the 8th and 10th centuries. The Lakshminarayan temple consists of a Vimana i.e. Shikhara and GarbhGriha with a small antarala. The Temple has a mandapa like structure also. The wooden Chhattries, the shell roof, a top the temple were in response to the local climatic conditions as a protection",
          "img": "/state-images/Himachal_Pradesh/Lakshminarayan-chamba.webp",
          "historical_significance": "9000-1000",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 31.139400114024394,
          "lon": 76.25892715507808
        },
        {
          "id": "m9ejqavl9",
          "title": "Himachal Pradesh- Monument- British colonial architecture Shimla",
          "name": "Himachal Pradesh- Monument- British colonial architecture Shimla",
          "desc": "Some of the world\u0092s finest examples of British colonial architecture can be seen in Shimla, the capital of Himachal Pradesh, which was once famous as the summer capital during the colonial period. The buildings like former Viceregal Lodge (now the Indian Institute of Advanced Study) made of greystone, the neo Gothic structures of the Gaiety theatre and the former imperial Civil Secretariat (now the Accountant General\u0092s Office) are some of the examples of the British architecture. Built in Gothic style, the Christ Church is the famous landmark on mall of Shimla which was built over a span of 11 years (1846-1857). The beauty of the church is enhanced by the stained glass windows (five in all) for which it is so famed.",
          "img": "/state-images/Himachal_Pradesh/shimla-architecture.webp",
          "historical_significance": "1800-1850",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 31.54073888779777,
          "lon": 76.76656570435577
        },
        {
          "id": "672bss6jx",
          "title": "Vajreshwari Devi Temple, Kangra",
          "name": "Vajreshwari Devi Temple, Kangra",
          "desc": "Objects are crafted with metal for religious, ritualistic and everyday needs of the people of Himachal. There are fine examples of freestanding metal statues at temple entrances in Brahmaur, Chamba and the Vajreshwari Devi temples in Kangra. Repousse technique was used to create beautiful temple doors. Low settees made of silver or brass are another common ritual artifact used in homes as well as temples, besides bells, incense burners, lamps, jars, flasks, tridents, fly whisks, and canopies. Brass is often used in the hills for fashioning household utensils. The Kinnaur metalsmiths also specialise in the repousse worked door handles fashioned in the form of crocodile, dragon or lion-head. All sacred buildings display these handles. Another interesting item is the dongbo or tea churner, as common as the intricately carved hookah bases.",
          "img": "/state-images/Himachal_Pradesh/vajreshwari-temple-kangra.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 32.05015718015603,
          "lon": 76.45476328144986
        },
