    {
      "id": "jammu-kashmir",
      "name": "Jammu & Kashmir",
      "geography": {
        "capital": "Srinagar",
        "key_cities": []
      },
      "facts": [
        "**Chashma-i- Shahi**: Mughal Gardens were the Emperors' concept of paradise. These were developed as a retreat from their capitals during the summer. They were adorned with terraced lawns, gushing fountains, bright flowerbeds with the panorama of the lake in front of them - the three Mughal Gardens of Chashma-i- Shahi, Nishat and Shalimar. Shalimar Bagh, built by Emperor Jehangir for his wife Nur Jehan, is a beautiful garden with sweeping vistas over gardens and lakes, and shallow terraces. The plan of the garden is based on the Persian charbagh design in which a square garden is divided into four equal parts by water channels which are lined with polished stones. The garden has four terraces, rising one above the other. The lowest was the public garden while the middle one was the Emperor\u0092s and the fourth terrace, by far the best, was reserved for royal ladies or the zenana. Nishat Bagh or the garden of bliss was designed in 1633 CE by Asaf Khan, brother of Nur Jehan during the reign of or the royal spring is a tastefully laid garden in terraces which was built by Shah Jehan. It is an exquisite garden laid around a natural spring."
      ],
      "art_forms": {
        "paintings": [
