        "dance_forms": {
          "folk": [
            {
              "id": "8d15b6f0",
              "name": "Santhali Dance - Jharkhand, West Bengal",
              "desc": "Linked with harvest & tribal festivals. This dance was performed by the Santhal tribes during all special festivals and occasions. The main attraction of the dance in Jharkhand are the colorful costumes worn by the tribal people. The typical garments worn by men during the Santhal are dhoti and a turban but the main attraction is their body decoration with various species of flora. The colorful environment of the festival is made more charming and attractive by the musicians who play the music on various folk instruments such as cymbals, pipes, flutes and drums. Moreover, the singers also put the right chord with the rhythm. After, the Bamboo folk dance of Assam and Mizoram, it is the Santhal dance of Jharkhand which is very popular among the tourists of north eastern region.",
              "img": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ef/Santali_cultural_dance_group_from_DSPMU_Ranchi_nfront_of_Jharkhand_Vidhansabha.jpg",
              "artists": "Kedar Nath Sahu \nKedar Nath Sahu was one of the leading exponents ofSeraikella Chhau. He comes from a traditional family of Chhau dancers\nbelonging to the Kanasari caste, who are engaged in these dance practices along with their main occupation of making household utensils. He started learning the dance form at a very early age from his father and grandfather. The dance form followed a Guru-Sishya Parampara and the art was orally transmitted from\ngeneration to generation. He performed in the troupe of Kumar Bijoy Pratap Singh Deo but later formed his own troupe with his disciples. Recipient of the Sangeet Natak Academy Award in 1981 and the Padma Shri in 2005. His performances received acclaim both in India and around the world. Many illustrious dancers\ntrained under him as his students; the notable ones being Shashadhar Acharya, Gopal Prasad Dubey and Sharon Lowen to name a few. (Ref: NCERT Textbook gekr1, Ch. 13)"
            }
          ],
          "classical": []
        }
