    {
      "id": "karnataka",
      "name": "Karnataka",
      "geography": {
        "capital": "Bengaluru",
        "key_cities": []
      },
      "facts": [
        "In Karnataka, the puppet theatre is said to have existed since ancient times. The two prominent forms of puppetry in the state are string puppets or Marionettes and Leather or Shadow puppets. At present many marionette troops exist in Karnataka in which the whole family works as a team. A string puppet means a puppet manipulated from above and controlled by long strings invisible to the audience. Made of wood, the puppets of North Karnataka are short and slim in appearance possessing a pair of legs and hands whereas the puppets of south Karnataka are three feet high but without legs. All puppets are controlled by strings and the strings are tied to the hands and the head of the puppet. Some of the puppet troupes have discarded strings and have replaced them with rods for easier manipulation of puppets.The usually consisting of a thick black backdrop, two or three wooden poles and a curtain to cover the puppet players from being seen by the audience. Prominent musical instruments used during the performance are mridangam (also known as Maddale in Kannada), mukhaveena (a unique wind instrument made of wood, measuring one feet long and consisting twelve holes), cymbals and a Harmonium. The puppeteers of Karnataka perform the dual role of a narrator and an operator simultaneously. They not only manipulate the puppets behind the curtains but also conduct dialogues. The puppet operator wear anklets and dance along with puppets but such actions are not visible to the audience. Leather puppetry is an ancient art which existed in the third century in Karnataka and Andhra Pradesh. At present, the leather puppetry is extensively found in Chitradurga, Bellary and Kolar districts. The puppeteers following this art are known as Gomberamas or Killekyatas. These troupes numbering forty are the only nomadic clan practicing this profession. The striking feature of shadow puppetry is that, they create a world of fantasy and colour just behind the screen. Their uniqueness lies in their shadow performance. In reality, in such a show, the actual puppet is concealed behind a screen and only its shadow is visible to the audience, requiring enormous skills to present a shadow puppet show. The leather puppets are made of either goat or deer skin which is painted with vegetable dyes. The leather puppets are flat structures and are transparent. The arms, legs and head of the puppet are movable. The musical narration is simple and vivid. The puppet show requires a simple stage. A white screen is important since it is behind this that the puppets are played and on which shadows of the performing puppets are cast. The puppeteers hide themselves from the audience's view behind a thick black blanket. A lamp is hung behind the white screen. The reflection of the light falls on the puppets whose shadows ultimately appear on the transparent white cotton screen."
      ],
      "art_forms": {
        "paintings": [
          {
            "id": "bd628058",
            "title": "Mysore painting",
            "name": "Mysore painting",
            "desc": "Mysore painting",
            "img": "/state-images-new/Karnataka/1b39265f7a85b2b27e6a222788659a50.jpg"
          },
          {
            "id": "6b66c12d",
            "title": "Chitrakala works",
            "name": "Chitrakala works",
            "desc": "Chitrakala works",
            "img": "https://images.indianexpress.com/2026/02/bengaluru-exhibition-express-photo.jpg?w=1200"
          }
        ],
        "performing_arts": [
          {
            "id": "ud1jw98bd",
            "title": "Karnataka- Performing Art- Yakshagaana",
            "name": "Karnataka- Performing Art- Yakshagaana",
            "desc": "Yakshagaana is believed to have a history of one thousand years and is believed to represent a specific type of music of the yakhas. Yaksha are the demi-gods attending on 'Kubera'- the god of wealth in the Hindu mythology. Gaana means song. By combining the meaning, the term Yakshagaana might mean the 'song of Yakshas'. Of the twenty districts in Karnataka, Yakshagana has established its firm roots in the districts of Dakshina Kannada, Uttara Kannada, Dharwad, Mysore and Hassan. The epics of Ramayana and Mahabharata offer extensive material for Yakshagana prasangas (or themes). The literature is presented both in verse and prose and the former is interpreted in simple prose and dialogues. The show begins with an announcement of Yakshagana by beating the drum, in important places of the village. Yakshagana is performed in a rectangular stage built with four wooden poles installed on four corners and covered by mats of palm leaves on the top. Away from the backstage is a small place called 'chowki', the greenroom for the artistes. The audience sits on all the three sides of the platform and a curtain covers only the back stage. In the Yakshagana , the Bhagavata is the chief narrator of the story, who recites it in verse form. Bhagavata also conducts light hearted conversation with the Vidushaka- the jester. The dialogues between these two characters is very amusing and the Bhagavata ably supports the Vidushaka in carrying out extempore dialogues. Music is the essence of Yakshagana and the narration is in verse form. It is the songs and poems which transmit the story and the songs are invariably sung by the Bhagavata. The music is excellent and synchronizes well with the costume and make up of the characters. Prose in Yakshagana is in the form of dialogues. The songs sung by the Bhagavata are at a very high pitch and hence is considered as a distinct style of music. The rhythm that dictates the body movements, footwork and the performance actually flows from the musical instruments.",
            "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/yakshagaana1.webp",
            "historical_significance": "",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "b456a6a4",
            "title": "Yakshagana",
            "name": "Yakshagana",
            "desc": "Yakshagana",
            "img": "/state-images/Karnataka/yakshagana4.webp"
          },
          {
            "id": "3988026c",
