      "monuments": [
        {
          "id": "328xyamhj",
          "title": "Padmanabhaswami Temple",
          "name": "Padmanabhaswami Temple",
          "desc": "This temple, dedicated to Padmanabha (Vishnu), is located inside the fort area or the old city in Thiruvananthpuram having a blend of architectural styles of Tamilnadu and Kerala. The entrance has a Gopuram which is a common feature from Tamilnadu while the other entrances with tiled roofs on wooden structures are typical of Kerala. The circumambulatory has a typical Nadambalam which is basically an enclosure for the temple with a wooden roof, latticed walls and carved pillars. The carvings depict the deepalakshmis or women holding lamps. The sanctum known as srikovil is double storeyed and square in shape. The mandapa is made of stone which again depicts the Tamilnadu style of architecture.",
          "img": "/state-images/Kerala/padmanabhaswamy.webp",
          "historical_significance": "",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 10.4544128571906,
          "lon": 77.31825721947042
        },
        {
          "id": "ml7ge79k9",
          "title": "Kerala- Monument- The Paradesi Synagogue-Kochi (Cochin)",
          "name": "Kerala- Monument- The Paradesi Synagogue-Kochi (Cochin)",
          "desc": "The Paradesi Synagogue The grand and glorious synagogue known as Pardesi synagogue named after the \u0093foreigners\u0094 who were the Jews from Spain and Portugal. This is one of the oldest synagogues built in the sixteenth century, being the only one in use even today out of the seven built in the Jew town. It was the king of Cochin, Paraja, who gave a piece of land to the Jewish community next to his palace to build the synagogue. It was reconstructed in 1662, after the Portuguese bombardment. Outside the synagogue, there are wrought-iron gates decorated with the Star of David. The rectangular building is white-walled and tile-roofed, with an inner courtyard lined with ancient Hebrew-inscribed grave stones. There is a Dutch-style square clock tower with four clocks featuring four different numerals: numerals facing the synagogue. Roman numerals, Malayalam numerals,and Arabic numerals facing the rest of thethree sides towards the public . At the bottom of the tower there is a well and inside the tower hangs the pendulum. The floor is paved with hundreds of old hand-painted porcelain tiles brought from China and no two of which are identical. The synagogue has a rich collection of gold and silver decorated Torah-scrolls crowned with solid gold and set with gems given as gifts by the kings, an oriental carpet and two brass columns symbolizing the pillars of the temple. In addition to this there is a collection of 10 paintings depicting the history of the Jews in the area.",
          "img": "/state-images/Kerala/paradesi-synagogue.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Medieval 1600-1650",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 9.967874,
          "lon": 76.2456743
        },
        {
          "id": "1e7wr6cl6",
          "title": "Mattancherry Palace",
          "name": "Mattancherry Palace",
          "desc": "The Mattancherry Palace is also known as the Dutch palace. It was built by the Portuguese in 1557 and presented to Raja Veera Kerala Varma of Kochi. The palace was renovated in 1663 by the Dutch and it took on its present popular name Dutch palace after 1663, when the Dutch carried out some extensions and renovations in the palace. The palace has two floors and a central courtyard which is in the traditional Kerala style of architecture known as nalukettus. The palace has a European character from the outside with scraped masonry walls and round-headed windows and doors but its sloping tiled roof and wooden balconies are local in features. The two arches at the entrance are typically Portuguese in character. Inside the inner court, there stands a small temple dedicated to Bhagavati, the protective goddess of the Kochi royal family. Two more temples are situated on either side of the Palace dedicated to Lord Krishna and Lord Siva. The interiors of the palace arepanelled with wood and has exhibits of the kings of Kochi like ceremonial robes, headdresses, weapons, palanquins, furniture but the main feature is the series of astonishing murals, depicting scenes from the Ramayana, the Mahabharata, and the Puranic legends connected with Shiva, Vishnu, Krishna, Kumara, and Durga painted on the walls as well as the portraits of the kings. The paintings cover a wide range of themes from the Puthra Kameshti Yagyam to Rama's return to Ayodhya after vanquishing Ravana. The paintings totally cover nearly 1000 sq.ft in area and date from as early as the 16th century through mid nineteenth century and are found in the chambers on the west side of the palace, in four chambers upstairs, and in two low ceiling rooms that are entered by a steep stairway. These beautiful and extensive paintings are fine examples of Kerala mural paintings best known for its unique style. The king's room is covered in about 48 paintings illustrating the Ramayana, from the beginning of the sacrifice of Dasaratha to Sita's return from captivity in Lanka. These paintings are the earliest in the palace, dating as early as 16th century. The last five scenes are from the \u0093Krishna Lila\u0094. Another important series of paintings in the Dutch palace is found in the upper rooms, notably that of the coronation hall that was made under Dutch patronage. Among the compositions are Lakshmi seated on the lotus, sleeping Vishnu (Ananthasayanamurti), Ardhanariswara and other goddesses, the coronation of Rama, Krishna lifting Mount Govardhana etc. The ladies chambers depict scenes from Kumarasambhavam and other works of the great Sanskrit poet Kalidasa. The dining hall has carved wooden ornate ceiling decorated with a series of brass cups. The palace also contains rare examples of traditional Kerala flooring, which looks like polished black marble but is actually a mixture of burned coconut shells, charcoal, lime, plant juices and egg whites.",
          "img": "/state-images/Kerala/mattancherry.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Medieval 1550-1600",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 9.9583508,
          "lon": 76.2591812
        },
