    {
      "id": "madhya-pradesh",
      "name": "Madhya Pradesh",
      "geography": {
        "capital": "Bhopal",
        "key_cities": []
      },
      "facts": [
        "**Pre-Historic ---- 3000 BCE**: Bhimbhetka has remained a landmark of generations of prehistoric art in India. It is believed that these paintings are twenty thousand to five hundred thousand years old. Bhimbetka lies south of Bhopal and is surrounded by the Vindhya ranges. Indian archaeologists date some of these paintings to the very early periods, including the Upper Palaeolithic period. But the statement has no reliable grounds. It can be dated rather to the Mesolithic. The Bhimbhetka Caves or rock shelters in Bhimbetka have ancient prehistoric paintings depicting the lives and times of people living in these caves. The scenes painted on the walls include animals around by them like wild buffalo, tigers, and rhinoceros. The scenes also depict activities from their lives like dance, religious rituals and childbirth. Other paintings depict the tools they used for cooking, hunting and other purposes. This makes the Bhimbetka group an archaeological treasure, an invaluable chronicle in the history of man and a World Heritage site. In Bhimbhtka, mineral and vegetable colours are primarily used. The colours used by the cave dwellers were prepared combining manganese, haematite, soft red stone and wooden coal. Sometimes the fat of animals and extracts of leaves were also used in the mixture. The colours have remained intact for many centuries due to the chemical reaction resulting from the oxide present on the surface of the rocks. The themes are taken from the everyday events depicting hunting, dancing, music, horse and elephant riders, animals fighting, honey collection, decoration of bodies, disguises and household scenes. Animals such as bisons, tigers, lions, wild boar, elephants, antelopes, dogs, lizards, crocodiles etc. have been abundantly depicted in some caves. The superimposition of paintings shows that the same surface was used by people of different times. Rock art, thus, gives tremendous clues to understand the life-style, entertainment or the mysticism prevalent in that era. The detailed concepts of animals from filled types to stick and x-ray types are found in abundance.",
        "**Jewellery**: Madhya Pradesh makes lac jewellery, the best known being in Rewa and Indore. In these places ornaments like chokers, earrings, rings, hair ornaments and large octagonal bead chains are made in traditional style. In this type of jewellery, lac is placed over the tin foil and melted so that the latter is fully covered by lac. This is largely used for ornaments which shine with a golden effect. One of the most attractive pieces made by this process is the anklet of clove shaped beads all cast in one piece called lauang kasauthi."
      ],
      "art_forms": {
        "paintings": [
          {
            "id": "l2bg90uz8",
            "title": "Wall paintings",
            "name": "Wall paintings",
            "desc": "The city of Orchha was founded on the banks of Betwa in the sixteenth century by the Bundela chief Rudra Pratap, who became the first ruler of Orchha. In the early seventeenth century, Raja Jujhar Singh rebelled against the Mughal emperor Shah Jahan, whose armies devastated the state and occupied Orchha from 1635 to 1641. The main historical monuments of Orchha are the Jehangir Mahal, Raj Mahal, Rai Praveen Mahal, Laxmi Narayan Temple and the Chaturbhuj Temple. The various palaces and temples built by the Bundela rulers retain much of their immaculate perfection. Of the succeeding rulers, the most notable was Raja Bir Singh who built the exquisite Jehangir Mahal, a tiered palace crowned with graceful Chhatries. Construction of the Raj Mahal was started by Rudra Pratap, and completed by one of his successors, Madhukar Shah. The Sheesh Mahal was built during the early 18th century, and was originally intended as an exclusive country retreat for the local king Udait Singh. It has two rectangular courtyards inside, the second, formerly used by the Bundela queens. Opulent royal quarters, raised balconies and interlocking walkways rise in symmetrical tiers on all four sides, crowned by domed pavilions and turrets. One can find the fragments of mirror-inlay and vibrant painting plastered over their walls and ceilings. Some of the friezes are still in remarkable condition. The Rai Praveen Mahal is a small, double-storeyed brick apartment built by Raja Indramani for his concubine in the mid seventeenth century. This building lies to the north of the Sheesh Mahal. The building, set amid the lawns of the Anand Mahal gardens, it has a main assembly hall on the ground floor, a private salon upstairs, and cool underground apartments. Entered through an ornate ceremonial gateway, the east-facing facade is covered with turquoise tiles. Two stone elephants flank the stairway, holding bells in their trunks to announce the arrival of the Raja. Three storeys of elegant hanging balconies, terraces, apartments and onion domes are piled around a central courtyard. This palace, however, is more airy and lighted since it has countless windows and pierced stone screens looking out over the skyline to the west, and a sea of treetops and ruined temples in the other direction.",
            "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/Orchha1.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 1500-1550",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "cvjq8xbtg",
            "title": "Fresco is a technique where the painting is done when the plaster on the wall is still wet therefore the colors intermingle permanently with the plaster and is permanent.",
            "name": "Fresco is a technique where the painting is done when the plaster on the wall is still wet therefore the colors intermingle permanently with the plaster and is permanent.",
            "desc": "Mural painting refers to paintings on walls, ceilings, or other large permanent surfaces. This style of painting can be traced back to prehistoric times such as the paintings on the Caves of Lascaux in southern France. The term Mural came into being with the Mexican muralista art movement. Fresco is a technique where the painting is done when the plaster on the wall is still wet therefore the colors intermingle permanently with the plaster and is permanent. For quite some time the art work was mistakenly referred to as \"frescoes\". We now know that the proper term for this kind of artwork is known as mural because the known process and technique of fresco painting isn't found in this kind of art. At Ajanta, the technique and process used to produce this kind of artwork is unlike any other artwork found in the art history of other civilizations. These murals have a uniqueness about them. The process of painting involved several stages. The first step was to chisel the rock surface, to make it rough enough to hold the plaster. The plaster was made of clay, hay, cowdung and lime. Differences are found in the ingredients and their proportions from cave to cave. While the plaster was still wet, the drawings were done and the colors applied. The wet plaster had the capacity to soak the color so that the color became a part of the surface and would not peel off or decay easily. The colors were referred to as 'earth colors' or 'vegetable colors.' Various kinds of stones, minerals, and plants were used in combinations to prepare different colors. Sculptures were often covered with stucco to give them a fine finish and lustrous polish. The stucco had the ingredients of lime and powdered sea-shell or conch. The latter afforded exceptional shine and smoothness. In cave upper six, some of it is extant. The smoothness resembles the surface of glass. The paint brushes used to create the artwork were made from animal hair and twigs.",
            "img": "",
            "historical_significance": "",
            "materials": ""
          },
