        "handicrafts": [
          {
            "id": "ph9k9dm25",
            "title": "Madhya Pradesh- Handicrafts \u0096Stonecraft",
            "name": "Madhya Pradesh- Handicrafts \u0096Stonecraft",
            "desc": "Balaghat has a lovely green stone out of which the local carvers make a number of small items, animals, boxes, trays, etc. But the important products are the religious images.",
            "img": "",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "mwg0ucqrx",
            "title": "Papier-mache Craft",
            "name": "Papier-mache Craft",
            "desc": "Madhya Pradesh is well known for papier-ma\u0302che\u0301 items. The main center for papier-ma\u0302che\u0301 is Ujjain but this craft is also popular in Gwalior, Ratlam and Bhopal. Craftsmen from Gwalior and Bhopal make statues of birds, animals and decorative panels. The craftsmen of Ujjain make papier-ma\u0302che\u0301 birds and figures of popular deities. These products",
            "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/papier-mache-mp.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "lmwvu9eys",
            "title": "Madhya Pradesh- Handicrafts -Toy Making",
            "name": "Madhya Pradesh- Handicrafts -Toy Making",
            "desc": "The tradition of making a special type of traditional rag dolls is prevalent in Gwalior.The faces of these dolls are painted beautifully and the eyes are painted in such a manner that they give them a very lively expression. The dolls are dressed in paper costumes in traditional style. The dolls are highly decorated and ornamented. These dolls are popularly known as battubai dolls as they were created by a woman named Battubai.",
            "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/ragdollsmp.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "0sq8ri69q",
            "title": "Madhya Pradesh- Handicrafts -Maheshwari Sarees",
            "name": "Madhya Pradesh- Handicrafts -Maheshwari Sarees",
            "desc": "The Maheshwari saree in Madhya Pradesh has an unusual origin for it is traced to a generous queen who once ruled over that territory and designed the first saree. Maheshwar, the place itself is a charming one on the banks of the river Narmada. The Maheshwari saree, mostly in cotton is characterised by its simplicity . The body is either plain or has stripes or checks with several variations. A speciality of the Maheshwari saree is its reversible border because of which either side of the saree can be worn. This is locally known as bugdi. The borders have a wide range of mat designs which consists of floral designs, the best known amongst which is the karanphool pattern.",
            "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/maheshwari.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "pi4hr18g8",
            "title": "Madhya Pradesh- Handicrafts- Bandhani",
            "name": "Madhya Pradesh- Handicrafts- Bandhani",
            "desc": "Madhya Pradesh has very unusual and attractive bandhanis or tie and dye work. The best known centres are two villages in western Madhya Pradesh on the opposite banks of river Gujari known as Tarapur and Umedpura. Mandsaur also produces some excellent bandhani. Amongst the wide selection of designs the most common is of chains of grains represented by dots on the body called the dana pattern while there are elaborate designs on the borders and pallus.",
            "img": "",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "7yrbgx74u",
            "title": "Madhya Pradesh- Handicarfts-Printed Textiles",
            "name": "Madhya Pradesh- Handicarfts-Printed Textiles",
            "desc": "The Malwa region of Madhya Pradesh has long being known for its printed cotton textiles which are printed in different techniques like direct dyeing and printing, resist dyeing and printing, rogan printing and screen printing. The important centres are Jawad, Bhairongarh, Tarapur, Umedpura and Sheopur. The communities engaged in this craft are chippa, rangrez and nilgar. Jawad was once famous for exporting the reknowned jazams to various countries under royal patronage.",
            "img": "",
            "historical_significance": "",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "7nmh8fzdf",
            "title": "Madhya Pradesh- Handicrafts-Durries",
            "name": "Madhya Pradesh- Handicrafts-Durries",
            "desc": "The durries of Madhya Pradesh, from Jobat and Tipgaon in the tribal district of Jhabua are well known for their sturdy character and delightful colours. Weaving in Jobat is simple, done on the pitlooms with panja, a woodden apparatus, almost like an extension of the hand, used to pull back firmly and fix the weft yarn in place. These mats are very attractive because of their delightful warm tints.",
            "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/durriemp.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
