      "monuments": [
        {
          "id": "d1yvkoh0o",
          "title": "Madhya Pradesh- Monument- Khajuraho",
          "name": "Madhya Pradesh- Monument- Khajuraho",
          "desc": "Known as the temple city of India, Khajuraho is known for its intricately carved, beautiful medieval temples. A symbol of India's glorious past, heritage and culture, the temples symbolize an immortal saga of Hindu art and culture in central India and are an outstanding image of the well developed Nagara style of temple architecture. Khajuraho was the historic capital of the Chandelas who built these temples from the ninth through the thirteenth centuries. Among the 85 original ones only 25 have survived the ravages of time. Around the temples are bands of fine work, portraying gods, goddesses, musicians, animals, etc. Erotic sculptures featuring apsaras and mithunas (couples) also dominate the themes portrayed here. shrine and four subsidiary shrines scattered around the landscape. The temples of Khajuraho are divided into three groups- the western,which is the largest group having temples dedicated to Shiva and Vishnu like Chitragupta Temple, Kandariya Mahadeo, Chausat Yogini Temple, Lakshmana Temple and Matangeswara Temple. The Eastern are the Jain temples including the temples of Pashvanath and the Adinatha which were built by the merchants and the Southern group, where the remains of the Vaidyanath Shiva temple has been exposed by the ASI (Archaeological survey of India). Khajuraho shows a highly evolved stage of Indian temple architecture. Most temples stand on a tall platform known as the Jagati. The elevation consists of three divisions: a pitha, the wall or jangha, and a roof or shikhara. Ornamentation is abundant on the basal storey in a series of mouldings. It depicts human activities, kirtimukhas and geometric designs. The wall section is divided into two or three sculptural sections including apsaras, mithunas and divine figures. As these figures rise upwards they add to the dramatic effect. The oldest of these temples, dating back to 900 CE is the Chaunsath Yogini temple, located southwest of the Shibsagar lake. Built of rough granite it is dedicated to the sixty four manifestations of the Mother Goddesses or the yoginis. This shrine is raised on a large platform in an open courtyard surrounded by miniature shrines for sixty four manifestations of the goddess. Kandariya Mahadeo temple is the largest in Khajuraho. It is considered to be the best representation of the nagara style of temples consisting of a garbhagriha or a sanctum, a circumambulatory path - pradakshinapatha, an antarala or the vestibule, ardhamandapa, mandapa and a mahamandapa. The sanctum enshrines a marble shivalingam. This mammoth structure stands 30 metres above the ground level. A rich variety of sculptures adorn the platform. They are sculptures of female figures in different poses engaged in writing letters, playing with a ball or applying makeup. The northern group has Vaishnava temples for the most part and the south eastern group consists of Jain temples. To the north of this temple is the Devi Jagadambi temple standing on the same platform as Kandariya Mahadeo. It is more delicately proportionate and the temple is decorated with some fine sculptures. Due to its smaller size, the sculptures are clearly visible. Further north is the sun temple, enshrining an image of the Sun God, on a chariot pulled by seven horses. To the extreme north of this, is the Vishwanath temple, built along the lines of the Kandariya Mahadeo temple. Inscriptions reveal that it was built by King Dhanga and that an emerald image of Shiva - Marakateswara was enshrined here. South west of this temple is the Parvati temple. The Lakshmana-Chaturbhuja temple stands on a platform which has a magnificent sculptural frieze of elephants and horse riders in a procession. There is also an embellishment of erotic sculptures. The Matangeswara temple enshrines Shiva, in the form of a highly polished gigantic Shivalingam. There is also a small Varaha temple with a gigantic monolithic image of a standing Varaha, the boar incarnation of Vishnu. The whole figure is carved in low relief. The main sanctum contains the image of Vaikunth Vishnu, bearing three heads representing his various incarnations. The eastern group has temples dedicated to Brahma. It is one of the smallest shrine with a single room having a shiv- linga protected by a huge pyramidal roof. Vamana and Javari are miniature versions of the western group of temples.",
          "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/chausath-yogini.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 750-1200",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 24.8515132,
          "lon": 79.9259786
        },
        {
          "id": "77o1dye25",
          "title": "Adinath Temple, Parsvanath Temple, Ghantai Temple, Hanuman Temple and Brahma Temple",
          "name": "Adinath Temple, Parsvanath Temple, Ghantai Temple, Hanuman Temple and Brahma Temple",
          "desc": "Among the temples in the eastern zone, the ones worth mentioning are: Adinath Temple, Parsvanath Temple, Ghantai Temple, Hanuman Temple and Brahma Temple, which can be divided into two, the temples dedicated to the Jain pantheon and the temples dedicated to Hindu Gods and Goddesses. The Jain temples include the Parsavanath Temple dedicated to Parsavanath, the Jain Tirthankara. It is a well decorated and the largest Jain Temple. Adinath temple is dedicated to Adinath, a Jain Tirthankara. The sculpted figures of Yakshis are particularly famous here. Ghantai Temple is well known for the sculpture of a Jain goddess on a winged Garuda and for the sixteen dreams of Mahavira's mother. The temples in the southern zone has Dulah Deo temple dedicated to Lord Shiva and Chattarbhuj temple. The Dulah Deo Temple has been dedicated to Lord Shiva. It is supposed to have been constructed between 1100 CE and 1150. Inside the temple there is a vast Shivalingam. There is also an image of Lord Shiva at the very entrance of the temple.The architectural complex consists of the mandapa, where the shrine is placed, along with the mahamandapa and the ardhamandapa with no circumbulatory passage. There is also the cluster of the shikharas, both subsidiary and centralized. The Chaturbhuja temple consists of the shrine of Lord Vishnu, to whom this is dedicated. This temple has also been constructed in the 1100. The structure is more or less similar to the Dulah Dao Temple. The exotic sculptures add to the glamour and elegance of the place.",
          "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/Adinatha-Temple.webp",
          "historical_significance": "",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 24.58898234924379,
          "lon": 77.17067342760488
        },
        {
          "id": "f6z9akftq",
          "title": "Chaturbhuj Temple",
          "name": "Chaturbhuj Temple",
          "desc": "Ram Raja Mandir, a palace-turned-temple has a charming legend attached to it. King Madhukar Shah brought an idol of Lord Rama from Ayodhya to his capital following the dream visitation of God Ram to be installed later in a temple. When the idol proved impossible to move, the king recalled, too late, the deity's edict that the image would remain in the place where it was first installed. It is also the only temple in India where Ram is worshipped as a king. Chaturbhuj Temple was built upon a massive stone platform and reached by a steep flight of steps. The temple was specially constructed to enshrine the image of Ram which, however, remained in the Ram Raja Temple. Lotus emblems and symbols of religious importance provide the delicate exterior ornamentation. Laxminarayan Temple A flagstone path links the Laxminarayan Temple with the Ram Raja Temple. The style here is a mix of fort and temple architecture. The interiors contain the most exquisite of Orchha's wall paintings. On the walls and ceilings of three halls, these murals are vibrant compositions and cover a variety of spiritual and secular subjects.",
          "img": "",
          "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 1550-1600",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 24.8251612,
          "lon": 79.9310377
        },
        {
          "id": "zigrrv3ny",
          "title": "Chanderi Fort",
          "name": "Chanderi Fort",
          "desc": "The Chanderi fort is located in the small historical town of Chanderi in the Ashoknagar District of Madhya Pradesh. The fort is the most striking monument in this ancient town and it dates back to the Mughal Period. The Muslim rulers, especially the Malwa Sultans took a lot of initiative to construct the fortification walls of the fort. The mahals within the fort were built by the Bundela rajputs revealing various phases of building styles. Located on top of a hill, the fortified walls enclose the citadel and protect it from outsiders. The Fort is approached through three gates. The first and main gate is well known as the Khooni Darwaza. It has been so named because criminals were thrown from the battlements above and their bodies crashed to pieces below. Hawa Paur is the third and highest gate of the Chanderi fort. The remnants of the Chanderi Fort include the Hawa Mahal and the Nau Khanda Mahal. Both these buildings were built by the Bundela chiefs. There is yetanother gateway on the south western side of the fort referred to as the Khatti Ghatti which is about twenty five metres in height forming part of the outer defenses of the town. There is a Purana Madarsa which was used as a school in the sixteenth century. It has latticed screens with geometric designs set in closed arches and the walls are embellished with lotus medallions. There is a massive structure known as the Koshak Mahal which houses four identical palace buildings which are situated at the corner of a square courtyard. The plain exterior contrasts well with the ornate bay windows. The battisi baoli is the biggest step-well in Chanderi. Thirty-two broad steps from each side descend down to a square pool.",
          "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/chanderifort-1.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 1500-1550",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 24.7067008,
          "lon": 78.1428974
        },
        {
          "id": "wp8xidw7k",
          "title": "Lal Baag Palace, Indore",
          "name": "Lal Baag Palace, Indore",
          "desc": "Lal Baag palace is located in Indore. It is surrounded by gardens and was built between 1886 and 1921. It is one of the grandest ancestral monuments built by the Holkar dynasty. The rooms have been restored and furnished and much of the furniture and ornamentation is in the early Georgian style. Its Italian marble columns, grand chandeliers, rich Persian carpets, flying nymphs on the ceiling, Belgian stained glass windows, Greek mythological reliefs, Italian style wall paintings speak of its past glory.",
          "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/lalbagh.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Modern 1850-1900",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 24.13332953269255,
          "lon": 78.15203454973833
        },
        {
          "id": "ykw39pel2",
          "title": "Madhya Pradesh- Monument- Indore-Kanch Mandir",
          "name": "Madhya Pradesh- Monument- Indore-Kanch Mandir",
          "desc": "As the name suggests, the full temple is made up of coloured glass. This Jain Temple was built by the Cotton King Sir Hukamchand in the early twentieth century. Inside this Jain temple thousands of mirrors adorn the walls, floor and ceilings, supplemented by brightly patterned ceramic tiles, Chinese lantern-type glass lamps and cut glass chandeliers. The use of glass beads and raised figures produces a pleasing three-dimentional effect. The paintings done in coloured glass depict stories from Jain scriptures.",
          "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/KanchMandir.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Modern 1900-1947",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 22.7163957,
          "lon": 75.849208
        },
        {
          "id": "gv31h4onv",
          "title": "Madhya Pradesh- Monument- Indore- Rajwada",
          "name": "Madhya Pradesh- Monument- Indore- Rajwada",
          "desc": "The Rajawada with its 7-storeyed gateway is a mixture of French, Mughal and Maratha styles. Its lofty entrance archway above a huge wooden door encrusted with iron studs, leads into a vast courtyard enclosed by galleried rooms. The lower three floors are made of stone and the upper floors are made of wood.",
          "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/rajwada.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Modern 1800-1850",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 22.7184344,
          "lon": 75.8547755
        },
        {
          "id": "sha34ynrx",
          "title": "Madhya Pradesh- Monument- Mandu",
          "name": "Madhya Pradesh- Monument- Mandu",
          "desc": "Mandu was built by the Paramara ruler Raja Bhoja and was once the capital of Malwa. It was built in the fifteenth century as a summer retreat. Jahaz Mahal is a unique monument in the shape of a ship which connects two lakes Kapur Talao and Munj Talao. Its unique construction shows the taste and the prosperous art and architecture of the past. Jahaz Mahal was built in fifteenth century when the place was under the rule of Ghiyas-ud-Din. Hindola Mahal means a swinging hall. It was built under the rule of Ghiyas-ud-din. The monument is an audience hall which is adorned with wonderful sculptures. The mahal was built with sand stone with beautifully carved columns. To the western front of the mahal there are few other mahals whose identities are not yet unveiled. Champa Baoli is a construction that stands in the midst of the Mahal depicting the past. There is a provision of hot and cold water that are also connected with the underground rooms.",
          "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/mandu-1.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 1450-1500",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 22.3397517,
          "lon": 75.4054509
        },
        {
          "id": "gjoqaf1mw",
          "title": "Gwalior fort",
          "name": "Gwalior fort",
          "desc": "Gwalior is an ancient city which was under the illustrious rule of the Scindias prior to India's independence. Gwalior was once known as Gopadri or Gopachala. It has relics from the period of the Guptas, Huns, Pratiharas, Tomaras, Pathans, Moghuls, Marathas and the British. Among the temples of interest at Gwalior are the Chaturbhuj temple, the Sas Bahu temple and the Teli Ka Mandir. The Chaturbhuj temple dedicated to Vishnu is located on the eastern road to the fort. It is a rock cut monolithic structure and it was built in the year 875 during the rule of the Pratiharas. The Sas Bahu temples are located on the eastern slope of the Gopadri hill. Both of these temples, built in the Nagara style of architecture are dedicated to Padmanabha or Vishnu. Ornately decorated, the doorways, ceilings, and the",
          "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/gwalior-fort1.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 750-1200",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 26.2235995,
          "lon": 78.1665381
        },
        {
          "id": "bbq1rnswd",
          "title": "Sas Bahu Temple",
          "name": "Sas Bahu Temple",
          "desc": "pillars are covered with intricate sculptural work.The hundred feet tall Teli Ka Mandir dates back to the ninth century and it is the highest building in Gwalior fort. Most of the decorative details in this temple follow the nagara idiom, while the tower is in the dravidian style.Near this temple Also in the fort is the tomb of Tansen, the musician who adorned the court of Akbar. Several Jain sculptures dating back to the fifteenth century adorn the slopes of the hill. Other attractions in the fort include the Gurjari Mahal built by Raja Mansingh during the",
          "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/saasbahumandir.webp",
          "historical_significance": "1500-1550",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 23.199813801488574,
          "lon": 77.94760422658226
        },
        {
          "id": "p9hny1z6o",
          "title": "Madhya Pradesh- Monument- Sanchi",
          "name": "Madhya Pradesh- Monument- Sanchi",
          "desc": "Sanchi, one of the most distinct archaeological sites in India is located right in the middle of the country. The world heritage site of Sanchi is being represented by various monuments including stupas, monolithic Asokan pillar, temples, monasteries and great sculptural wealth built over several centuries from the early Mauryan period to the twelfth century. It was Emperor Asoka who laid the foundations of a religious centre at Sanchi by erecting a monolithic pillar. He erected the stupa 1 here after redistribution of mortal remains of Lord Buddha for erecting several stupas all over the country in order to spread Buddhism. This stupa was originally a low structure of bricks, half the diameter of the present edifice hemispherical in shape with raised terraces at the base. It was enclosed by a wooden railing and a stone umbrella at the top. This great stupa later served as a nucleus to the large Buddhist establishment. Ashoka built a total of eight stupas on the hilltop of Sanchi including the great stupa. A number of stupas and other religious structures were added over the succeeding centuries. With the decline of Buddhism, the site decayed and was eventually completely forgotten. But, between1912 and 1919, the structures were rediscovered and carefully restored to their present condition. Originally stupas were mounds covering the relics of the Buddha or his followers. In its earliest stages Buddhist art didn't represent the Buddha directly. Instead, his presence was alluded to through symbols such as the bodhi tree, the wheel of law or his footprint. The stupa also became a symbol of the Buddha. More exactly, it became a symbol of his final release from the cycle of birth and rebirth - the Mahaparinirvana. During the Sunga period, several edifices were raised at Sanchi and its surrounding hills. The Ashoka stupa was enlarged and faced with stones and decorated with balustrades, staircases and a harmika on the top. In the Ist century BCE the Andhra-Satavahanas, whose boundaries covered the Malwa region, added the elaborately carved gateways to stupa 1 which is the most elaborate and well known of more than fifty magnificent Buddhist monuments that crown the hilltop of Sanchi. A nearly perfect hemispherical dome, the great stupa1 is topped by a triple parasol set within a square railing or harmika. A third of the way up from its base, a raised terrace, enclosed by a fence, is meant for ritual circumambulation of the monument. A second, stone-paved procession-path at ground-level is enclosed by an encircling stone balustrade. This path is accessed from the cardinal directions through four exquisitely carved gateways. The exquisite carvings of the doorway depict in detail the significant episodes and miracles from Lord Buddha\u0092s life and events depicted in the Buddhist jataka stories. The eastern gateway depicts the young prince, Gautama leaving his father's palace on his journey towards enlightenment and the dream his mother had before his birth. The western gateway depicts the seven incarnations of the Buddha. The northern gateway crowned by a wheel-of-law, this depicts the miracles associated with the Buddha as told in the Jatakas. The southern gateway shows the birth of Gautama revealed in a series of dramatically rich carvings. From the second to fourth century CE Sanchi and Vidisha came under the rule of Kushanas and Kshatrapas and subsequently passed on to the hands of the Guptas. During the Gupta period some temples were also built and sculptures were added displaying the classical grace and simplicity of the era. Further, statues of Lord Buddha seated in the canopies facing the four entrances of the great Stupa were also added. Sanchi also flourished during the seventh century when shrines and monasteries were continued to be added. Thus Sanchi displays harmonious co-existence of Hindu and Buddhist faiths.",
          "img": "/state-images/Madhya_Pradesh/sanchi.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Ancient 600-001 BCE",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 23.4876339,
          "lon": 77.7360676
        },
