        "handicrafts": [
          {
            "id": "2xnwwyvl4",
            "title": "Maharashtra- Handicrafts- Kolhapuri Chappals",
            "name": "Maharashtra- Handicrafts- Kolhapuri Chappals",
            "desc": "Kohlapuri chappals are handcrafted leather sandals that get their name from the place of their origin, the district of Kohlapur. Intricate patterns are handcrafted and made of leather; the interesting fact is that even the cords used to stitch the sandals are made of leather and no nails are used. The hide of bulls, cows, and goats are used for the leather. Kachkadi, bakkalnali, and pukari are some traditional designs. Kohlapuri chappals range from those with simple straps and plain colours and designs to more decorative ones with a gold cord or gota on them. These chappals, which were probably worn only by the inhabitants of Kohlapur, are now worn all over India and abroad.",
            "img": "",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "irl3rfzhd",
            "title": "Maharashtra- Handicrafts- Lacquerware",
            "name": "Maharashtra- Handicrafts- Lacquerware",
            "desc": "Savantvadi in Ratnagiri district is famous for its unique, brightly coloured lacquered toys and dolls, imitation fruits and vegetables. The best known is a set with a man and a woman and a toy winnowing fan in folk style. They mainly show men and women in either standing or sitting postures. Hale and pangora wood is used by the traditional craftsmen who are known as chittorees (picture makers). At one time this was a big craft centre for the round lacquered highly pictorial playing cards known as ganjifa.",
            "img": "",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "uqfek09w5",
            "title": "Maharashtra- Handicrafts- Paithani Silk Sarees",
            "name": "Maharashtra- Handicrafts- Paithani Silk Sarees",
            "desc": "A very beautiful style of saree called paithani named after the village Paithan is a well known handicraft of Maharashtra. On a zari wrap thread the weft is interlocked with different colours. In this technique, complicated patterns of flowers and birds are woven in bright colours. Asavali (vine and flowers), Narli (coconut design) and petal design are the few traditional motifs. Due to the closeness of Paithan to the famous Ajanta caves, there are motifs of Buddha woven standing or in the sitting posture and motifs of gold coins are also represented in the weaves. The pallu is an exquisite piece of gold tissue around which are woven brightly coloured rosettes and birds.",
            "img": "",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "m8e8ifvf5",
            "title": "Maharashtra- Handicrafts- Tusser Silks",
            "name": "Maharashtra- Handicrafts- Tusser Silks",
            "desc": "Ganeshpur in Bhandara district is famous for tusser silk weaving. It is the main occupation of the Koshti community which is half the working population of this district. They claim to be descendents of sage Makaranda known to have woven the first fabric from lotus fibre to clothe the gods. Tusser silk produced here is known by its Sanskrit name kosa. Some varieties of tusser silk produced in this area are motha choukada - a design in big squares, lahan choukada - a design in small squares, gunja salai - diagonal designs on cloth along with coloured lines at regular intervals in green, blue or orange, teen dhari choukada -- the three square pattern, and rasta choukada -- square pattern with horizontal lines running through.",
            "img": "",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "tlz937rol",
            "title": "Maharashtra- Handicrafts-Weaving- Mashru and Himru",
            "name": "Maharashtra- Handicrafts-Weaving- Mashru and Himru",
            "desc": "Aurangabad is famous for Mashru and Himru fabrics. These two types of fabrics are made of cotton and silk having a shine like satin. The ornamental design is formed on the principle of extra weft figuring. The extra silk weft left loose makes the Himru shawl soft, which almost feels like silk. Weavers were brought to Aurangabad from Banaras and Ahmedabad by Mohamed-bin-Tughlak and thus the Himru industry started.",
            "img": "",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
