      "monuments": [
        {
          "id": "z73c2anxm",
          "title": "Aurangabad- Monument- Kailasanatha Temple at Ellora",
          "name": "Aurangabad- Monument- Kailasanatha Temple at Ellora",
          "desc": "A marvel of engineering and artistry Kailasanatha temple dates back to the period of the eight century Rashtrakuta period. It is a collosal monolithic rock cut temple built in the Dravidian style and is known for its architectural grandeur as well as its sculptural splendour. More than thirty temples have been carved from the hillside at Ellora during the period sixth to ninth centuries CE. Its excavation must rank as an architectural wonder. Two great trenches some ninety meters long were dug into the hillside. They were connected at the deepest point by another trench fifty three metres across. The temple was meant to appear as though rising from a vast courtyard at ground level. The great block of residual rock, rising thirty metres, was then carved into the three- storeyed vimana, the main mandapa, two giant dhwajasthambas or pillars, and four sub- shrines. Beginning at the top, the mass of rock was hewn into shape, and as each layer was shaped, the carvers began ornamenting the structure moving downward, thus eliminating the need for any scaffolding. The temple is dedicated to Shiva and named after his abode in the Himalayas, the Kailasa. It was built during the period 757 and 773 CE. The temple covers a large area and the vimana (tower) rises to a height of about ninety feet. The most amazing thing to be emphasized over and over again and seen to be believed is that the entire monument was made by excavation and not by construction. The typical Dravidian features includes a gateway, an enclosure for the Nandi, and a mandapa lined up in front of the sanctum which is crowned with a vimana, composed of successively receding storeys, decroated with miniature buildings. The mandapa in front of the sanctum is an enormous pillared hall with elaborate carving. On either side of the Nandi mandapa are two fifty feet high obelisk like pillars decorated with frieze carvings. Two storeys of corridors have been carved into the mountain, ringing the temple on three sides. These corridors are studded with small alcoves, all containing a wealth of sculpted figures telling the tales of the great Hindu epics, the Mahabharata and the Ramayana. Among the narrative friezes is the descent of river Ganga, and one of Ravana shaking Mount Kailasa. The architectural style and intricate sculpture is similar to Virupaksha Temple at Pattadakal that had been completed a decade before. A collonaded gallery running along the bottom of the cliff face forms a deep narrow passage that surrounds the temple. In between this cliff face and the temple is the pradakshinapatha. The temple tower resembles the rathas at Mamallapuram near Chennai in Tamilnadu. The Kailasanatha temple resembles the architectural style established by the Pallavas of Mamallapuram and is yet considerably grander in scale. Other caves of interest in Ellora are the Rameshwar cave, the Sita ki Nahani cave, and several Jain caves such as the Indra Sabha cave, all dating back to the period of sixth-ninth centuries CE.",
          "img": "",
          "historical_significance": "Early Medieval 750-1200",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 20.462711169551774,
          "lon": 76.90791697015108
        },
        {
          "id": "dv7n9k2x7",
          "title": "Mumbai- Monument- Chhatrapati Shivaji Terminus (formerly Victoria Terminus)",
          "name": "Mumbai- Monument- Chhatrapati Shivaji Terminus (formerly Victoria Terminus)",
          "desc": "The Chhatrapati Shivaji terminus, formerly known as Victoria Terminus in Mumbai was designed by Frederick William Stevens. It is an outstanding example of Victorian Gothic architecture in India, blended with themes deriving from Indian traditional architecture. The terminal was built over ten years starting in 1878 according to a design based on late medieval Italian models. Its remarkable stone dome, turrets, pointed arches, and interesting ground plan are close to traditional Indian palace architecture. The interiors are adorned with wood carving, tiles, ornamental iron and brass railings, grills for the ticket offices, and the grand staircase with balustrades.",
          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/victoria-1.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Modern 1850-1900",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 19.336150890513093,
          "lon": 76.41200886935742
        },
        {
          "id": "5dwqlaz0v",
          "title": "Mumbai- Monument- Gateway of India",
          "name": "Mumbai- Monument- Gateway of India",
          "desc": "The Gateway of India, a massive archway on the Apollo Bunder, is one of the grand and magnificent landmarks of Mumbai, which was built to commemorate the visit of the British Monarch, King George V and Queen Mary. The Gateway of India is one of the hot spots of Mumbai city. Designed by the architect George Wittet it was formally opened in 1924 by the then Viceroy, Earl of Reading. The Gateway is a 26m high structure with four turrets and complicated lattice work engraved into the yellow basalt stone. The central dome of the Gateway of India is 48 feet in diameter. It is interesting to note that the whole harbour front was realligned in order to come in line with a planned walkway, sweeping down to the centre of the town. At the back of the Gateway arch, steps lead down to the sea front.",
          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/gateway-1.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Modern 1900-1947",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 18.9219661,
          "lon": 72.8345657
        },
        {
          "id": "lo8mh07vp",
          "title": "Mumbai- Monument- Elephanta",
          "name": "Mumbai- Monument- Elephanta",
          "desc": "The Elephanta Island is located ten km away from the Gateway of India at Mumbai. These caves house rock cut temples dating back to the fifth century CE. Elephanta Island was so named by the Portuguese, after the statue of an elephant near the landing area of the island. There are six caves dating back to sixth and seventh century. These rock cut temples dedicated to Lord Shiva are abundant in sculptural content. Created by carving out rocks, and creating columns, internal spaces and the images, the entire temple can be compared to a huge sculpture. Some of the rock surfaces are highly finished while some are untreated bare rock. The entire cave temple consists of a main chamber and two lateral ones, courtyards and several subsidiary shrines. Above the temple is the mass of natural rock. There are three entrances to this temple with the one on the east and the west marking the axis of the temple. A twenty pillared hall lines the axis, and on its western end is the cella in which is enshrined a Shiva linga. The pillars consist of fluted columns standing on square bases, and are crowned with fluted cushioned capitals. The most enigmatic image is of Trimurti or the Sadasiva manifestation of Shiva which is a collossal twenty feet high image of the three headed Shiva. Also on the southern wall are grand sculptured images of Kalyanasundara, Gangadhara, Ardhanariswara and Uma Maheswara. To the west of the northern entrance are sculptured images of Nataraja and Andhakaasuravadha urth, and to its east are images of Yogiswara and Ravanaanugrahamurthy in eight manifest forms. At Elephanta caves, Shiva is portrayed in the non-anthropomorphic Shivalinga form, as well as in the colossal human image. The secondary shrine is located to the east of the main temple in a courtyard. This temple contains six pillars at its entrance, four of which are free standing and two connected. The entrance leads to a hall decorated with sculptured panels depicting legends from the Shiva Purana.",
          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/elephanta-1.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Ancient 001-600 CE",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 18.96341,
          "lon": 72.9313593
        },
        {
          "id": "a5616056r",
          "title": "Aurangabad- Monument- Bibi Ka Makbara",
          "name": "Aurangabad- Monument- Bibi Ka Makbara",
          "desc": "Bibi Ka Maqbara, the burial place of Aurangzeb's 1st wife, Rabia-ud-Durrani is an imitation of the Taj at Agra and is often referred to as the poor man\u0092s Taj Mahal due to its poor copy of the masterpiece. The mausoleum is made mostly of sandstone and has plastered walls and a marble dome.The tomb is adornedwith lattice work in marble and inlay decorations of flowers and creepers which are beautiful. A high wall with bastions runs around the monument and the recesses have little minarets. The entrance leads to an arch and from this point, a fine view of the structure ahead may be obtained. The pavement that leads to the mausoleum is flanked by oblong reservoirs.",
          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/bibi-ka-makbara.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Medieval 1650-1700",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 19.9014476,
          "lon": 75.3202331
        },
        {
          "id": "e7rs0iefn",
          "title": "Daulatabad Fort",
          "name": "Daulatabad Fort",
          "desc": "Once known as Devagiri, this place was later renamed as Daulatabad by the then Sultan of Delhi, Mohammed - bin - Tughlak. One of the world's best preserved forts of medieval times the fort has hundreds of secret passages. The fortress was built to be secure with three massive walls to defend it against enemy attacks. The fort has some impressive structures like the Chand Minar, Jami Masjid and Royal Palace which are worth mentioning. The palace also has a few spacious halls, courtyards and pavilions.",
          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/daulatabad-1.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Ancient 600-001 CE",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 19.9425936,
          "lon": 75.2128587
        },
        {
          "id": "9708q5n2j",
          "title": "Mumbai- Monument- Kanheri Cave",
          "name": "Mumbai- Monument- Kanheri Cave",
          "desc": "Kanheri caves were the viharas for the Buddhist monks during the period between second century BCE and ninth century CE. These caves are simple and bare, quite different from that of Elephanta caves which are artistically embellished. Kanheri caves are mostly tiny cells that are cut into the edges of a hill, each having a stone plinth that might have served as a bed. There is also a congregation hall supported by huge stone pillars that contains the dagoba, a kind of Buddhist shrine. The largest is the Cave 13 which is adorned with sculptures in front of the stupa.",
          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/Kanheri-Caves.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Ancient 001-600 CE",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 19.206605,
          "lon": 72.906515
        },
        {
          "id": "2ig1g5lq2",
          "title": "Lonavla- Monument- Bhaja Cave",
          "name": "Lonavla- Monument- Bhaja Cave",
          "desc": "The Bhaja caves near Lonavla are the perfect examples of the phase of early Buddhist architecture. Lord Buddha has been represented here through numerous symbols in the caves. However, the caves which were carved after the fourth century CE, also presents the Buddha in his physical forms. The pillars of these caves are carved with figures of flowers and other images. The cave 12, is a Chaitya hall, which is the largest of all has a fine vault. The last cave towards the south is a good example of fine architecture and sculpture. This cave includes the figures of a dancing couple, a prince seated on an elephant and The remaining 7 caves contain inscriptions about the donors. The chaitya caves do not have a wall in the front as they are open which might have been enclosed by a wooden door of some kind in olden days. The patterns of the ceilings are prototypes of wooden ceilings.",
          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/bhaja-1.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Ancient 600-001 BCE",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 20.540384465466854,
          "lon": 76.29431909562841
        },
        {
          "id": "ze7foe2ct",
          "title": "Karle- Monument- Karle Cave",
          "name": "Karle- Monument- Karle Cave",
          "desc": "The Buddhist caves at Karle, built by the Buddhist monks, are the epitome of ancient rock-cut caves to be found in India. Dating back from the second to first century BCE, the Karle caves in Lonavla, were built during the Satvahahana period and hosted the monks in the monsoons, Built from top to bottom in the form of a ladder, the early Karle caves do not depict the Buddha in his physical forms but rather in symbols. However, from the seventh century CE when the Mahayana became more popular with their liberal thoughts, the later Karle caves do present the Buddha in his physical form such as the Buddha preaching while seated on a lion-supported throne, along withother magnificent carvings. The caves comprise mainly two types of structures: chaitya halls and viharas. The chaitya halls, which form the largest number of chaityas in the world (one being one hundred and forty eight feet long), were used for congregational worship whereas the viharas were the dwelling place for the monks and further included cells cut into the walls around three sides of a hall used for their residential and individual meditation. The pillars of the halls inside the caves have been carved with figures of men, women, flowers and elephants, though the pillars outside these halls have lions erected on them as their crowns, an emblem of Ashoka, the great Indian ruler.",
          "img": "/state-images/Maharashtra/karle-1.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Ancient 600-001 BCE",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 18.6694203,
          "lon": 72.9006289
        },
        {
