        "dance_forms": {
          "folk": [
            {
              "id": "559e4c27",
              "name": "Pung Cholom",
              "desc": "Performed at festivals combining dance & drumming. The Pung, or Manipuri drum is the soul of Manipuri dance. The Pung Cholom dance is performed during the festival of Holi. The Pung Cholom or Drum Dance is a visual interpretation of the various rhythmic patterns played on the pung. In this dance, the drummer identifies completely with the intricate rhythms he plays on the drum and expresses it through corresponding body movements and footwork. The Pung Cholom is part of the music of the Sankirtan tradition and is acclaimed as one of the best art forms of the state. \n\nIn the Pung Cholom dance of Manipur, the drummers jump and spin holding a drum. (Ref: NCERT Textbook gekr1, Ch. 11)",
              "img": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6c/Cholom.jpg",
              "artists": ""
            }
          ],
          "classical": [
            {
              "id": "c27a5bd1",
              "name": "Manipuri",
              "desc": "Manipuri is a classical dance from Manipur in NorthEast India. This dance form is soft, gentle and flowing. The movements are smooth and round, almost like gliding. The dancers always look calm and peaceful. The dance includes raas leela (stories of Krishna) and traditional movements from Lai Haraoba. Manipuri dance also includes Sankirtan, a devotional music and dance form performed with cymbals and drums, often as part of temple rituals. The famous costume is the poloi dress\u2014a stiff, colourful skirt with a veil and elegant jewellery. The men wear white dhotis and turbans, especially during pung cholom or raas performances.",
              "img": "/downloaded_images/e0746c42c0053ec31c10c97a4e414a40.webp",
              "images": [
                "/downloaded_images/e0746c42c0053ec31c10c97a4e414a40.webp",
