        "dance_forms": {
          "folk": [
            {
              "id": "8aca4e6e",
              "name": "War Dances -  Angami, Ao, Sumi tribes \u2013 Warrior tradition, Konyak, Lotha, Chakhesang dances",
              "desc": "Sumi war dance - The Ao Naga tribe from the northeastern part of Nagaland celebrates the Moatsu Mong festival as a time of recreation and joy, after the hard work of clearing fields, burning jungles, and sowing seeds. It is marked by vibrant songs, dances and merrymaking. Another major festival, Tsungrem Mong, is observed on the eve of the harvest to offer gratitude to the Supreme Power, with colourful costumes, songs and dances. The Ao Naga have a rich tradition of clothing that distinguishes between commoners and warriors, with the Ao warrior known as mangkotepsu. The Sumi tribe performs the energetic Sumi war dance to celebrate victory in battle, while the Lotha women perform the mungyanta, a harvest dance during the Tokhu Emone festival. Nagaland is home to around 30 traditional dances, including the Sangtam War Dance, Hornbill Dance, Chang Lo and Butterfly Dance, each reflecting the cultural richness of its many indigenous communities.\nOne of the important persons who has contributed a lot to the dances of the community is Sangyusang Pongen of the Ao community. He received the Sangeet Natak Akademi Award for his contribution. (Ref: NCERT Textbook hekr1, Ch. 11)",
              "img": "",
              "artists": ""
            }
          ],
          "classical": []
        }
      },
