          {
            "id": "da006a49",
            "title": "Odisha) (Ref: NCERT Textbook gekr1, Ch. 8)",
            "name": "Odisha) (Ref: NCERT Textbook gekr1, Ch. 8)",
            "desc": "Odisha) (Ref: NCERT Textbook gekr1, Ch. 8)",
            "img": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Odissi_dance_performance_26.jpg/640px-Odissi_dance_performance_26.jpg"
          }
        ],
        "performing_arts": [
          {
            "id": "zmml7dn4t",
            "title": "Odissi dance",
            "name": "Odissi dance",
            "desc": "The Odissi dance is the classical dance of Orissa. It has a long, yet unbroken tradition that may be traced back to more than 2000 years. Dancers are found depicted in bas-reliefs in the hills of Udaygiri dating back to the first century BCE. The Natya Shastra speaks of the dance from this region and refers to it as Odra-Magadhi. Most notable people who popularised Odissi dance in the recent times are Guru Deba Prasad Das, Guru Pankaj Charan Das, Guru Raghu Dutta, and Guru Kelu Charan Mahapatra. There are a number of characteristics of the Odissi dance. The style may be seen as an accumulation of aesthetic and technical details. The Tribhangi is one of the most characteristic features of Odissi dance The concept of Tribhang divides the body into three parts, head, bust, and torso. Any posture which deals with these three elements is called tribhangi.",
            "img": "/state-images/Orissa/odissidance.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "4pswelxbh",
            "title": "Guru Kelucharan Mohapatra dancer",
            "name": "Guru Kelucharan Mohapatra dancer",
            "desc": ", guru and promoter of Odissi dance was born in Raghurajpur, a village near the temple town of Puri. He learnt to paint and sculpt at an early age and started playing in folk theatre groups at the age of nine. In 1994, he set up Srijan, an organisation dedicated to train students in Odissi dance. He was instrumental in projecting Odissi dance on the world stage in a big way in the early 70s and took this classical art form to a great level, His students include Sanjukta Panigrahi, Madhavi Mudgal, Protima Bedi and Sharon Lowen. Recipient of Padma Vibhushan, the second highest civilian award he died in Bhubaneshwar in April 2004. The themes of Odissi are almost exclusively religious in nature. They most commonly revolve around Krishna. The ashtapadis of Jayadev\u0092s Gita Govind are a very common theme. There are a number of musical instruments used to accompany the Odissi dance. One of the most important is the pakhawaj, also known as the madal. Other instruments which are commonly used are the bansuri (bamboo flute), the manjira (metal cymbals), the sitar and the tanpura. With numerous religions, ancient temples, local shrines, tribes and an array of sacred places, Orissa observes uncountable number of festivals and fairs round the year. Orissa has a large number of temples built in Nagara style of architecture, with distinctive features specific to this region. The three major temples Lingaraja temple at Bhubaneswar, Jagannath Temple at Puri and the Sun Temple at Konark represent the complete evolution of this style of temple architecture during the tenth century, was the peak of architectural activity. A temple in Orissa is known as a deul and it consists of a sanctorum, one or several front porches known as Jagamohana usually with pyramidal roofs, a dancing hall or the Nata Mandir and a hall of offerings called a Bhog Mandir.",
            "img": "/state-images/Orissa/kelucharan.webp",
            "historical_significance": "1900-1947",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
