        "handicrafts": [
          {
            "id": "n22usblp8",
            "title": "Punjab- Handicrafts- Embroidery- Phulkari",
            "name": "Punjab- Handicrafts- Embroidery- Phulkari",
            "desc": "Craft forms an integral part of the lifestyle of the people of Punjab. Phulkari, is an art of embroidering shawls, dupattas with floral motifs, dress materials etc. It is done on handspun, handwoven coarse dyed khadi with an ordinary needle and strands of untwisted silk. It is a skillful art of a single stitch that provides interesting patterns on the cloth. The smaller the stitch, finer is the quality of the embroidery. The silk threads in golden yellow, red, crimson, orange, green, blue, and pink are usually employed for the embroidery. The notable aspect of this technique is that single strand was used at a time, each part worked in one color and the varied color effect is obtained by clever use of horizontal, vertical or diagonal stitches. In Phulkari the design is worked very closely that even a square inch of the base cloth is not visible. It is then called \u0091Bagh\u0092. Besides floral motifs, birds, animals, human figures, vegetables, pots, buildings, rivers, the sun and the moon, scenes of village life, and other imagery are embroidered. Some of the designs include dhaniya bagh (coriander garden), motia bagh (jasmine garden), satranga bagh (garden of rainbow), leheria bagh (garden of waves) and many other depictions. It is made by female members of the house, and takes a long time to make; sometimes even a few years. Traditionally, a Phulkari shawl is gifted to the daughter at her wedding. Another kind of shawl is the Chope, It is very simple in comparison to the Phulkari and Bagh, and is presented to a new bride by her maternal grandmother. The darshan dwar shawl is gifted to a temple by a devotee whose wish has been fulfilled.",
            "img": "/state-images/Punjab/PHULKARI.webp",
            "historical_significance": "1600-1650",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "50pxi7jau",
            "title": "Punjab- Handicrafts- Woodwork",
            "name": "Punjab- Handicrafts- Woodwork",
            "desc": "At Kartarpur, Jalandhar and Hoshiarpur, craftsmen create pidhis which are low, four-legged woven stools. They are carved out from wood, and then covered with lacquer and woven with threads of different colors. Other lacquer ware products include table lamps and dolls. Motifs and ornamentation are drawn from life around them -Patta (leaf), dodi (bud), jhari (bush), flowers and animals and birds-are some of the common ones.",
            "img": "",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "7qacq1uh0",
            "title": "Punjab- Handicrafts- Leather",
            "name": "Punjab- Handicrafts- Leather",
            "desc": "Leather jootis (shoes and slippers) of Punjab are extremely well known. Rich gold and multi-colored threads are used to decorate and impart a royal touch to a variety of jootis crafted from leather of different shades. In many parts of Punjab, entire families continue to devote themselves to making jootis.",
            "img": "/state-images/Punjab/leather-jootis.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
