    {
      "id": "sikkim",
      "name": "Sikkim",
      "geography": {
        "capital": "Gangtok",
        "key_cities": []
      },
      "facts": [
        "Monasteries are mostly known as Gompas. But Gompa literally means \"a place of solitude\", a religious escape where monks are iner aspects of Tibetan Buddhism. Most gompas in Sikkim are found in solitary places since isolation from the world has always been an important aspect of Buddhist learning merely as an escape from worldly temptations. Monasteries usually occupy commanding and frequently picturesque sites. The monastic buildings cluster around the main building which is also used as the Assembly Hall or du-khang. Rows of prayer flags and chortens are lined up along the path to Gompa.",
        "**Carpet Weaving**: The women of Sikkim practice what is perhaps the oldest form of carpet weaving in the world. The tradition is essentially Central Asian and a frame loom is used for the purpose. The women of the Bhutia community are believed to be expert weavers. The warp is prepared from cotton and is stretched on a frame. Different colors of wool are introduced into the pattern and so are a number of rods. The patterns commonly woven onto the carpets are a dragon holding a ball in his mouth or stylized floral motifs. Patterns are based on Buddhist motifs and figures. Geometrical patterns are popular too. Most patterns draw heavily on the surrounding beauty of the landscape."
      ],
      "art_forms": {
        "paintings": [
          {
            "id": "j3a3fmh50",
            "title": "Sikkim- Handicrafts- Thangka Paintings",
            "name": "Sikkim- Handicrafts- Thangka Paintings",
            "desc": "Known as thangkas, the typical paintings of Sikkim, were originally a medium of reverence through which the highest ideals of Buddhism were evoked. Thangkas are created on cotton canvas and often framed with silk. They depict images of different gods, goddesses, and philosophies related to Buddhism. Traditionally, thangkas were made by Buddhist priests and monks and some specific ethnic groups. The skills were passed from generation to generation. The art has also helped the monasteries and its practitioners keep it alive. There are three types of thangkas. One depicts the life of Buddha, his birth, his disillusionment with life, his search for enlightenment and his understanding of life. The second type of thangka is more abstract and represents the Buddhist beliefs of life and death. One example could be the Tibetan Wheel of Life, which symbolizes the Buddhist belief of transmigratory existence. The third type of the thangka consists of paintings that are often used as a tool for meditation or as offerings to the deities. These paintings are usually done against a white background. The colors used are all vegetable dyes and each has a special significance. White stands for serenity, golden for the birth or life, enlightenment, and parinirvana, red for the intensity of passion both love and hatred, black for anger, yellow for compassion and green for consciousness. The colors used in making a thangka are all vegetable or mineral dyes extracted from nature.",
            "img": "",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
