        "dance_forms": {
          "folk": [
            {
              "id": "80a5881f",
              "name": "Singhi Chaam",
              "desc": "Masked dance performed during Buddhist festivals. Mount Khan-chen Dzongpa (Kanchanjunga) is the third highest mountain in the world. This towering creation of nature is sacred to the people of Sikkim. It has been decreed the guardian deity of Sikkim by Guru Rimpoche (Guru Padmasambhava). This majestic mountain stands guard over the land holding in its crevices the sacred mountain peaks known as the Five Treasures. Its associate peaks look like the legendary snow lion, fierce and fiery, with a gorgeous mane hued in turquoise. The snow lion is considered an important cultural symbol of the state and is elaborately depicted in the 'Singhi Chham' or the Snow Lion Dance. The snow lion is a mythical animal. The Sikkimese people believe that if one sights the animal it brings good luck. This cultural symbol of good luck comes alive in this dance.",
              "img": "/downloaded_images/438f4e3429d02eafbb8874639b81856f.webp",
              "artists": ""
            }
          ],
          "classical": []
        }
      },
      "monuments": [
        {
          "id": "n1zinpue9",
          "title": "Monument-",
          "name": "Monument-",
          "desc": "Literally meaning rock-cave, there are four important Tak-Phu of special importance, one in each direction. These are four sacred caves where Guru Rinpoche (Padmasambhava or the Second Buddha) and Lhatsun Chhembo are said to have halted and meditated. These are namely Lha-ri nying phu, Kah-do Sang phu, Pe-phu and De-chhen phu.",
          "img": "",
          "historical_significance": "",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 27.6016108,
          "lon": 88.4550204
        },
        {
          "id": "f7ytizwmg",
          "title": "Sikkim- Monument- Monasteries - Rumtek Gompa",
          "name": "Sikkim- Monument- Monasteries - Rumtek Gompa",
          "desc": "Back in the 1950\u0092s when the Chinese invaded Tibet, His Holiness, the Gyalwa Karmapa \u0096 the sixteenth Karmapa and head of the Kagyu-pa (Black Hat) order of Buddhism, came to live in Sikkim. In the 1960\u0092s, he constructed the three-storeyed Rumtek Gompa. It is believed that the monastery is an excellent replica of the original Kagyu-pa Monastery in Tsurphu, Tibet. It remains the biggest monastery in Sikkim, and is a fine example of Tibetan architecture. The latest Karmapa who fled Tibet in the year 2000, now resides in the Rumtek Gompa. It of the world's most unique religious scriptures, manuscripts and religious art objects. The large prayer-hall in the monastery is especially noteworthy for its exquisite murals, statues and tangkhas (painted or embroidered scrolls).",
          "img": "/state-images/Sikkim/Rumtek_Monastery_Buddha1.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Modern 1947-1980",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 28.120061181052012,
          "lon": 88.6852154206675
        },
        {
          "id": "vc7zwn8wn",
          "title": "Sikkim- Monument-",
          "name": "Sikkim- Monument-",
          "desc": "The Phodong Gompa is an early eighteenth century edifice and the monastery belongs to the same order of Buddhism as the Rumtek Gompa, and was the most important monastery of the order before Rumtek came up. Though the monastery is much smaller and less",
          "img": "/state-images/Sikkim/PhodongMonasteryX1.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Medieval 1700-1759",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 27.6016108,
          "lon": 88.4550204
        },
        {
          "id": "ibfbxtndn",
          "title": "Sikkim- Monument- Monasteries - Labrang Gompa",
          "name": "Sikkim- Monument- Monasteries - Labrang Gompa",
          "desc": "The Labrang Gompa, which belongs to the Nyingmapa (Red Hat) Order of Buddhism is a monastery which was built about a 100 years after the Phodang Gompa. From the monastery one can see the ruins of the third capital of Sikkim \u0096Tumlong, which became the capital in the early nineteenth century and remained so for about 90 years. Only the chortens (a dome-shaped structure that contains the relics of the Buddha or other Buddhist saints) that surround the palace are still somewhat recognizable.",
          "img": "/state-images/Sikkim/labrang-gompa.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Modern 1800-1850",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 28.384314850667575,
          "lon": 89.0189389174037
        },
