        "dance_forms": {
          "folk": [
            {
              "id": "16801a56",
              "name": "Hozagiri Dance",
              "desc": "While the theme of the dance remains almost to be the same as of other tribes, the dance form of the Reang community is quite different from others. The movement of hands or even the upper part of the body is somewhat restricted, whereas the movement beginning from their waist down to their feet creates a wonderful wave. Standing on an earthen pitcher with a bottle on the head and a lighted lamp on it, when the Reang belle dance twisting rhythmically the lower part of the body, the dance bewilders the onlookers. The Reangs also use the musical Instruments like Khamb, Flute made of bamboo and bamboo cymbal. The Reang women prefer to put on black Pachra and Rea. Reang women put on coins ring, which generally covers their entire upper region. They also put on rings made of coin in their ears. They are fond of fragrant flowers as ornaments to metal things.",
              "img": "/downloaded_images/2ee639081773470d0844d051809449bb.webp",
              "artists": ""
            }
          ],
          "classical": []
        }
      },
      "monuments": [
        {
          "id": "gtws0ajdc",
          "title": "Ujjayanta Palace",
          "name": "Ujjayanta Palace",
          "desc": "is the royal house, which stands in the Capital city of Agartala. Built by Maharaja Radha Kishore Manikya during 1899-1901, this two storied royal house is beautifully carved and sculpted in Indo-Greek style. High domes, well laid out gardens and parks, fountains and tanks make it an impressive structure. The palace is set with huge Mughal style gardens, beautified by pools and gardens and tiled floors. Particularly notable are the magnificently tiled floors, the ceiling of the Chinese Room which was crafted by artisans brought from China, and the carved front doors. The two-storied palace has three large domes, the largest of which is 86 ft (26 m) high from the ground and which rests a top a four-storied central tower.",
          "img": "/state-images/Tripura/btr0100004.webp",
          "historical_significance": "",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 23.8369868,
          "lon": 91.2827507
        },
        {
          "id": "ok4uahqcf",
          "title": "Tripura- Monument- Rock Cut Carving in Tripura",
          "name": "Tripura- Monument- Rock Cut Carving in Tripura",
          "desc": "Tripura has beautiful rock cut carvings and stone images at Unakoti, Debtamura and Pilak. Most of these carvings are huge in size and made on vertical walls exposed in the open atmosphere.",
          "img": "/state-images/Tripura/Pilak.webp",
          "historical_significance": "",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 24.50741405359148,
          "lon": 92.15157538600494
        },
        {
          "id": "palxqw5tz",
          "title": "Unakoti Palace",
          "name": "Unakoti Palace",
          "desc": "Unakoti dating back to the eight or ninth century probably has the biggest bas-relief sculpture in India. The Legend- According to Hindu mythology, when Lord Shiva was proceeding to Kashi along with a crore of gods and goddesses he made a night halt at Unakoti. Shiva asked all the gods and goddesses to wake up before sun rise and proceed to Kashi. In the morning no one except Lord Shiva woke up, forcing him to set out for Kashi by himself and cursing the others to become stone images. Lord Shiva's curse fell upon them and as a result there are one less than a crore stone image carvings at Unakoti. The images found at Unakoti are of two types namely rock-carved figures and stone images. The most imposing sculpture is the immense 30 feet-high image of Lord Shiva called Unokotiswara Kal Bhairava carved in a vertical rock face. The colossal image in grey stone has an elaborate head. On each side of the central Shiva\u0092s head-dress are two full size female figures - one of Goddess Durga standing on a lion and another female figure on the other side. In addition to this, three enormous images of Nandi the bull vahana of Lord Shiva are found half buried in the ground. A small, rock strewn stream flows near the central Shiva. Near the huge Shiva image, among scattered rocks, are the three imposing images of Lord Ganesha. There is a huge figure of the four - armed seated Ganesha and on its side two standing figures of Sarabhuja Ganesha with three tusks and the Asthabhuja Ganesha with four tusks- a rare sight to behold. Among the rock cut carvings, the central Shiva and gigantic Ganesha are certainly master pieces. There are various others stone as well as rock cut images at Unakoti.Every year a big fair popularly known as Ashokastami Mela is held here in the month of April which is visited by thousands of pilgrims.",
          "img": "/state-images/Tripura/unakoti.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Early Medieval (750-1200)",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 24.6083250874031,
          "lon": 91.50448702265855
        },
        {
          "id": "i8psgoond",
          "title": "Tripura- Monument- Palaces- Debatamura",
          "name": "Tripura- Monument- Palaces- Debatamura",
          "desc": "Debatamura is famous for its panels of rock carvings on the steep mountain wall on the bank of Gomati. There are huge images carved of Shiva, Vishnu, Kartika, Mahisasur Mardini, Durga and other gods and goddesses. This is also called Chabimura. These images dating back to fifteenth-sixteenth centuries are carved with a lot of dexterity on the rocky faces of Debtamura which is as steep as 90 degrees.",
          "img": "/state-images/Tripura/btr0160001.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Medieval (1450-1500)",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 23.490914018991877,
          "lon": 92.25733702296947
        },
        {
          "id": "sc1o0ysbl",
          "title": "Tripura- Monument- Palaces- Pillak",
          "name": "Tripura- Monument- Palaces- Pillak",
          "desc": "A treasure house of Hindu and Buddhist sculpture dating back to 8th and 9th centuries, Pillak boasts of beautiful images scattered in an area of about 10 Sq.Km. The terracotta plaques, stone images and seals found here reflect the existence of both Hinduism and Buddhism. Colossal stone images of Avalokiteshwar and Narasimha have also been found here. Communities of Tripura The Tripuris represent more than 50% of the total tribal population of the State. The Tripuris live on the slopes of hills in a group of five to fifty families. Tripuri women wear a skirt, called Pachra, which reaches down just below the knee. They weave in their loin-loom a small piece of cloth, which they call Risha, and they use this small piece of cloth as their top garment. Next to Tripuris, the Reangs constitute the second biggest group among the tribal population. People of",
          "img": "/state-images/Tripura/Pilakbishnu.webp",
          "historical_significance": "",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 23.195535290998222,
          "lon": 92.04054023695872
        },
