      "monuments": [
        {
          "id": "9463vhuvb",
          "title": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Bara Imambara, Lucknow",
          "name": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Bara Imambara, Lucknow",
          "desc": "Asaf-ud-Daulah, an enthusiastic builder of monuments shifted his capital to Lucknow from Faizabad. Driven with an ambition to testify his architectural zeal, the Bara Imambara was built by this generous Nawab, who employed more than 22,000 people, including nobles. He adopted a dual policy of construction. During the day, the Imambara was built, and at night, the workers destroyed parts of what had been built. This was to ensure that no one remained unemployed, and the edifice was not completed too soon. This is indeed a monumental feat considering the fact that it was once the largest vaulted hall in the world, with a 50 feet high roof, spanning an area of 162 feet. The galleries and corridors within the great Imambara form a complicated and intricate labyrinth (bhool-bhuliaya) through which at times it is difficult to find one\u0092s way.",
          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/bada-imambara.webp",
          "historical_significance": "1750-1800",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 26.8686449,
          "lon": 80.912496
        },
        {
          "id": "r15ba86jc",
          "title": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Rumi Darwaza",
          "name": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Rumi Darwaza",
          "desc": "Towards the west of the Imambara is the Rumi Darwaza or the Turkish Gate built. The 60 feet high gateway stands as an equally grand entrance to the great hall. During the Nawabi era, it is believed that a huge lantern placed atop the Rumi Darwaza would light up the pathway, while jets of water gushed from the numerous fountains created on the rim of the gateway.",
          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/rumi-darwaza.webp",
          "historical_significance": "1750-1800",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 26.8712234,
          "lon": 80.9122124
        },
        {
          "id": "90i738478",
          "title": "Qaiser Bagh Palace",
          "name": "Qaiser Bagh Palace",
          "desc": "flanked by two mermaid gates (Lakkhi gate) at eastern and western ends. On the right end was Chandiwali Baradari, which it is believed was paved with sliver and the Khas Muqam and Badshah Manzil, were the special residences of the king. During the days of the mutiny Begum Hazrat Mahal held court from here. The buildings at the Qaiser Bagh quadrangle were occupied chiefly by ladies of the Harem. One of his favourite wives Nawab Mashuq Mahal used to live in it.",
          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/kaiser-bagh.webp",
          "historical_significance": "",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 26.64107512699068,
          "lon": 81.50548595521857
        },
        {
          "id": "th9vk3usk",
          "title": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Residency",
          "name": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Residency",
          "desc": "Asaf-ud-Daula\u0092s brother Sadat Ali proved to be a great builder who introduced a large number of architectural styles. One of his best-known monuments is the Residency, which was built for the British Residents. The ruins of the Residency stand desolate as a mute witness to the Mutiny of 1857 when it was almost completely destroyed. The redbrick ruins and the shattered walls are still scarred by cannon shots. The cemetery at the nearby ruined church has the graves of 2000 men, women and children. The 1857 Memorial Museum at the Residency has been established and designed to present a visual accounts of the freedom struggle of 1857. Located in a portion which was annex of the main Residency building, original photographs and lithographs of many sites, connected with the uprising, and paintings depicting significant events of the uprising have been displayed along with portraits of local heroes. Period objects such as guns, swords, shields, cannons, rank badges, medals and other items also form part of the display.",
          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/residency.webp",
          "historical_significance": "",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 26.8616593,
          "lon": 80.9257979
        },
        {
          "id": "4mv81wz9q",
          "title": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Chhota Imambara",
          "name": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Chhota Imambara",
          "desc": "The British crowned the third king of Awadh, Muhammad Ali in 1837 at a ripe old age of 63. He was a just and popular ruler under whom Lucknow once again regained its splendor for a brief spell. Interested in building activities, he built his own Imambara as well as the Juma Masjid. The Chhota Imambara as it had been called later was left incomplete by Muhammad Ali, was later completed by Begum Mallika Jehan of the Royal family. It has a golden dome, exquisite chandeliers, huge mirrors, silver minbar, colourful interiors and delicate calligraphy on its arched entrance.",
          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/Chota-Imambara.webp",
          "historical_significance": "1800-1850",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 26.8736758,
          "lon": 80.9043365
        },
        {
          "id": "d8qj41axu",
          "title": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Tomb of Itmad-ud-Daula",
          "name": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Tomb of Itmad-ud-Daula",
          "desc": "Tomb of Itmad-ud-Daula is one of the most attractive structures in Agra. Designed by Nur Jahan the favorite queen of Jahangir, for her father, the structure is renowned for its pietra dura decoration i.e. the inlaid stonework and the carvings. It is the first Mughal building to be constructed of white inlaid marble, a type of architecture which becomes characteristic of Mughal buildings in the seventeenth century. Believed to have inspired the design of the Taj Mahal, the tomb stands on a low platform and is rectangular with four minars or domed towers growing out of its four corners. The roof is flat, and at the centre is a small roofed pavilion. The marble fac\u0327ade is inlaid with patterns in multi-coloured stones and colourful intarsia representations of vases with flowers, wine cups, cypresses and fruit trees, while much of the second storey is composed of exquisitely carved pierced screens.The structure is set within a formal charbagh and is approached by a monumental gate.",
          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/Itmad-Ud-Daulah-Tomb.webp",
          "historical_significance": "1600-1650",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 28.9580986,
          "lon": 77.3307037
        },
        {
          "id": "vvwpznne7",
          "title": "Uttar Pradesh- Agra- Monument- Taj Mahal",
          "name": "Uttar Pradesh- Agra- Monument- Taj Mahal",
          "desc": "Overlooking the River Yamuna, the Taj Mahal was built by Emperor Shah Jahan to enshrine the body of his favourite wife, Arjumand Banu Begum, better known as Mumtaz Mahal who died shortly after giving birth to her fourteenth child, in 1631. The layout follows a distinctly Islamic theme representing Paradise. The walled complex is approached from the south through a massive arched gateway topped with delicate domes and adorned with Koranic verses, directly aligned with the Taj. Ustad Isa of Turkey is credited to have been the main architect along with Ismail Afandi, who built the domes. Chiranji Lal was called from Delhi to pattern the mosaic while Amanat Khan was the master calligrapher from Persia. Stone cutters came all the way from Baluchistan. Square in shape, the Taj Mahal known for its symmetry, sits on a raised platform surrounded by four minarets. The fully developed four minarets mark an important stage in Mughal architecture. The most remarkable feature of these minarets is their harmony with the tomb. The Taj surmounts a square marble platform marked at each corner by a high minaret. It is topped with a huge central dome , a bulging white double - dome with a broad padma-kosa (lotus - petals) and beautiful Kalasa-finial mounted on the tomb, capped by a crowning brass spire. It is embellished with extraordinarily fine detail of relief carving, highlighted by floral patterns of precious stones. Carved vases of flowers including roses, tulips and narcissi, rise subtly out of the marble base, a pattern repeated more colourfully and inlaid with precious stones around the four great arched recesses on each side. Chhatris flank the dome on all the four angles which appear to be attached to the dome when viewed from all sides. At the Taj the builder calculated the distance between the two features for the precise relationship they were intended to present together. The plan, which is also known as panch-ratna gives the Taj Mahal such a remarkable, impressive and unparalleled grace. The marble mausoleum is square in plan with chamfered corners. Each facade of the tomb is composed of a grand iwan framed by bands of calligraphy. The doorways inside these iwans are also adorned with calligraphy and is flanked on both sides by small double arches one over the other. Each section in the facade is crowned by beautiful pinnacles with lotus buds and finials. The 99 names of Allah adorn the top of Mumtaz's tomb, and set into Shah Jahan's is a pen box, the hallmark of a male ruler. These cenotaphs, in accordance with Moghul tradition, are only representations of the real coffins, which lie in the same positions in an unadorned and humid crypt below. The pietra dura or inlay has been very well combined with the jali-work. The inlay work on the spandrels of the arches in the interior and exterior add magnificence to the overall aesthetic effect. Bouquets of flowers, zigzag motifs and series of small arches are some of the most commonly used decorative elements. Slender twigs, twisting leaves and bold flowers combine to give the monument a beautiful look. Contemporary texts specify the stones used in decoration and inlay work they are- Lapis lazuli, various types of jasper, agate, carnelian, jade, amethyst, turquoise, onyx, coral etc. The Makrana marble with its pearly clarity has contributed most to the beauty of the Taj Mahal. A number of marks are engraved on red stone slabs, on the pathways, stairs, plinths and pavements of the Taj Mahal. Some of them are- symbolic motifs swastika, chakra, aako, a (Hexagon), Paucako,a (Pentagon), aakh (conch-shell), animated motifs - fish, bird, geometrical motifs - triangle, square, rectangle, floral motifs- leaves and petals of the flowers chiefly lotus. The Taj stands at the end of superb gardens designed in the charbagh style so popular among the Persian architects. Divided into four quadrants by waterways, they evoke the Islamic image of the Gardens of Paradise, as according to the holy Koran, a garden is symbolic of paradise where rivers flow with water, milk, wine and honey. The \"rivers\" converge at a marble tank in the centre that corresponds to al-Kawthar, the celestial pool of abundance mentioned in the Koran. Another genius of the architect is reflected in the layout of the fountains and somber rows of cypress trees, which are present only on the north-south water canal, lest the attention of the onlooker would be diverted to the sides.",
          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/taj_mahal_01.webp",
          "historical_significance": "1650-1700",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 27.1750075,
          "lon": 78.0421013
        },
        {
          "id": "s14gymzje",
          "title": "Agra Fort",
          "name": "Agra Fort",
          "desc": "Built by the Mughal emperor Akbar, the Agra fort contains numerous impressive structures like the Jahangir Mahal, Khas Mahal, Diwan-i-Khass, Diwan-i-Am, Machchhi Bhawan and Moti Masjid. Imprisoned in the Agra fort by his son Aurangzeb, Shah Jahan spent his last days in the Musamman Burj of the Agra fort. The Musamman Burj is located on the left of the Khaas Mahal. It is a beautiful octagonal tower with an open pavilion. The Jahangiri Mahal was built by Akbar and is one of the few structures that survives from his reign. It is a stately building with rows of ornate pillars with elaborate hanging brackets set around an open courtyard. One of the most beautiful complexes in the fort is the Khaas Mahal constructed by Shahjahan. The central area has a marble courtyard, a rectangular pool with frilled edges and a fountain. The Diwan-e-Khas boasts of Shahjahan\u0092s evolved and mature style, with cusped arches and rich lattice marble screens and veneers.",
          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/agra-fort.webp",
          "historical_significance": "1600-1750",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 27.1798258,
          "lon": 78.0208223
        },
        {
          "id": "r0e7r8wfa",
          "title": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Fatehpur Sikri",
          "name": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Fatehpur Sikri",
          "desc": "Fatehpur Sikri was a planned town by Akbar with great care and planning in the honor of Sheikh Salim Chisti but had to be abandoned only after fifteen years due to scarcity of water in the town. Sheikh Salim Chishti was a Sufi saint who blessed the Akbar with his first son and heir, who was named Salim after him and later came to be known as Jehangir. The town was originally named Sikri after the village, where it was founded but its twin city Fatehpur (Victory Town) was erected to celebrate Akbar's conquest of Gujarat. This complex includes Jodha Bai's palace, Hawa Mahal, Birbal's House, Stables, Panch Mahal, Mariyam's House, Zenana Garden, Nagina Masjid, and the Imperial Harem. This was the private place for the women of the palace. This place was designed keeping in mind the purdah custom for women; still the place is well lighted and well ventilated. Buland Darwaza at Fatehpur Sikri was built by Akbar to commemorate his victory over Gujarat. Made of red and buff sandstone, decorated by carving and inlaying of white and black marble and approached by 42 steps it is the highest gateway in the world and an astounding example of the Mughal architecture. Jami Masjid, one of the most elegant and sacred building is situated on the highest point of the ridge where Fatehpur Sikri is situated. The Tomb of the famous Sufi saint Sheikh Salim Chishti are a part of this spacious mosque complex. Built in the supervision of the saint himself and his descendants, it was completed in 1571. The vast quadrangle has the mosque on the western side. The main rectangular mosque consists of a central nave with a single dome, two colonnaded halls on its either side with two square chambers crowned with dome. The elegant and beautifully carved mehrabs adorn the main chamber and the two smaller rooms. The mosque marks the phase of transition in Islamic art as the indigenous architectural elements were blended with the Persian architecture. The pillared dalan of the facade, the iwan with three arched openings also framed by panels and crowned by five chhatris and the central mehrab adorned with inlaid mosaic of stones that are bordered by glazed tiles and has golden inscriptions on royal blue background. The interiors of the iwan are adorned with watercolor paintings depicting stylized floral designs. Even the dado panels, spandrels of arch, squinches and soffits are also painted profusely. Unlike other monuments, where domes are supported on squinches, here elegant corbelled pendentives support the dome. Tansen's Baradari Known as 'Barahdari' as it has twelve doorways, this single-storeyed red sandstone building is rectangular in shape with three doorways on each side. The arched doorways grace its northern and southern sides while the eastern and western sides have flat-roofed doorways. Though, people believe that Tansen, one of the Nine Gems of Akbar's court who was a famous musician lived here, the idea seems to be too absurd to be true. Not only the building is too small for a nobleman's house with only a single oblong hall, it has no attachments that are a must in a residential house such as hammams or baths, toilets or room for the ladies. Akbar's court had Navratnas or Nine Jewels, a term denoting a group of nine extraordinary people who have since become a part of Akbar's legend. They were: Birbal who was a brilliant administrator famous for his great wit and justness. Bhagwant Das, the Commander-in-Chief of the Mughal Armies. Man Singh, one of Akbar's most trusted Generals. Todar Mal, the empire's Finance minister. Tansen, beloved court singer of Akbar. Abul Fazl, the official State Historian of Akbar's reign and author of Akbarnama, the court chronicle of the Emperor Akbar. Abdul Rahim Khanikhana, a renowned poet in Persian, Sanskrit, and Hindustani. Aziaodin, a sage and one of the chief advisors of the Emperor, along with the other Navratnas. Poet Faizi who is best known for his Nal u Daman, a poetic rendering of the Sanskrit story of Nala and Damayanti.",
          "img": "",
          "historical_significance": "1550-1600",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 27.0911655,
          "lon": 77.6623984
        },
        {
          "id": "ovok9gmq4",
          "title": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Ghats of Varanasi",
          "name": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Ghats of Varanasi",
          "desc": "With more than 100 ghats along the Ganga some of the prominent and popular Ghats at Varanasi are the Dasaswamedh Ghat, Manikarnika Ghat, Harischandra Ghat, Kabir Ghat and Assi Ghat. Dasaswamedh Ghat is one of the most important Ghats of Varanasi. Dasaswamedh literally means the river front of ten sacrificed horses where ten times the Ashwamedha Yagya has been performed by different kings. Manikarnika Ghat is the main cremation ghat of Varanasi. Manikarnika Ghat is one of the oldest and most sacred ghats in Benaras. Hindus believe that who ever is being cremated here after the death becomes free from the cycle of births. Tulsi Ghat is named after the great Hindu poet of the sixteenth century, Tulsidas, who composed the, Ramcharitmanas sitting at this ghat in Varanasi. Perhaps, to commemorate this, a temple of Lord Rama was built on the Tulsi Ghat. Many of the relics of Tulsisas are preserved at the Tulsi Ghat. The house in which Tulsidas died has been preserved and his samadhi, wooden clogs, pillow and the idol of Hanuman, which Tulsidas worshipped, are all still intact here.",
          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/tulsidas.webp",
          "historical_significance": "1947-1080",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 27.19312815775239,
          "lon": 80.36523643597381
        },
        {
          "id": "jmct93foa",
          "title": "UttarPradesh- Monument- Sarnath",
          "name": "UttarPradesh- Monument- Sarnath",
          "desc": "About 10 km from the holy city of Varanasi, is the Sarnath where Buddha delivered his first sermon after gaining enlightenment. The mantra, Buddham Sharanam Gachhami, owes its origin to Sarnath. On the day before his death Buddha included Sarnath along with Lumbini, Bodh Gaya and Kushinagar as the four places he thought to be sacred to his followers. It makes Sarnath one of the most venerated Buddhist places. Besides Buddhism, Sarnath is also connected with Jainism. Some of the important Buddhist monuments at Sarnath are the Dhamekha stupa, the Chaukhandi stupa and monasteries and temples of different schools of Buddhism from Japan, China, Thailand, Burma etc. The Indian Buddhist society called Mahabodhi Society maintains a deer park around the Buddha temple. The Mahabodhi Temple within the park has a tooth relic of the Buddha. The capital of Ashoka pillar of Sarnath is the national emblem of India. Chaukhandi Stupa is one of the important Buddhist Stupas at Sarnath. It is a lofty mound of brick, whose square edifice is surrounded by an octagonal tower. It was built to mark the place where Lord Buddha met his firstdisciples as he traveled from Bodh Gaya to Sarnath. The Dhamekh Stupa is the most remarkable and massive structure at Sarnath. Built partly in stone and partly in brick, the stone facing the lower part is decorated with delicate floral carvings of Gupta period. Dhamekh Stupa at Sarnath as it signifies the place where Buddha delivered his first sermon.",
          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/sarnath.webp",
          "historical_significance": "100-600 CE",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 25.3776274,
          "lon": 83.0275999
        },
        {
          "id": "t98phlruw",
          "title": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Kapilvastu",
          "name": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Kapilvastu",
          "desc": "Piprahwa 22 km from Siddharthnagar is identified with ancient Kapilvastu where Lord Buddha spent the early years of his life. Kapilvastu was the capital of the Shakya clan whose ruler was the father of Lord Buddha, and therefore Lord Buddha is also called as Shakyamuni. Prince Gautam, as the Buddha was then known, left his palace in Kapilvastu at the age of 29, and only to come back after 12 years, after attaining enlightenment. Today, Kapilvastu comprises of several villages, mainly Piprahwa and Ganvaria. A large Stupa stands at the ancient site which is said to have housed the bone relics of Lord Buddha. The presence of these relics is testified by an ancient Brahmi inscription found at Piprahwa. The ruins of the royal palace of King Shuddhodhana, the father of Prince Gautam are spread over a large area It was at this place, where the Shakya prince Siddhartha Gautama had spent 29 years of his life before leaving home to become the Buddha.",
          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/kapilavastu.webp",
          "historical_significance": "",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 27.2699457,
          "lon": 83.0750046
        },
        {
          "id": "qf0tapg2p",
          "title": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Kapilvastu Stupa Complex",
          "name": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Kapilvastu Stupa Complex",
          "desc": "This is the main archaeological site which was discovered during excavations. The seals and inscriptions over the lid of the pot discovered read Om Deoputra Vihare Kapilvastu Bhikschu Mahasanghasa and Om Deoputra Vihare Kapilvastu Bhikschu Sanghasa. The title Deoputra refers to Kanishka, a great patron of Buddhism who built the biggest Vihara at Kapilvastu and renovated the main stupa here.",
          "img": "",
          "historical_significance": "",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 27.113750172555832,
          "lon": 80.98904686488264
        },
        {
          "id": "mw0eu62vc",
          "title": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Kushinagar",
          "name": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Kushinagar",
          "desc": "Kushinagar, one of the principal centres of the Buddhist pilgrimage, is the place where Lord Buddha attained Maharparinivana \u0096 gained freedom from rebirth. The Chinese travelers Fa Hien and Hieun Tsang have also mentioned Kushinagar in their travelogues. The credit for bringing this ancient site to light goes to General A. Cunningham and A.C.L. Carlleyle who, after excavating the site in 1861, established its antiquity for the first time. Later, more",
          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/kusinagar.webp",
          "historical_significance": "",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 26.7438272,
          "lon": 83.9162322
        },
        {
          "id": "oohgnzufj",
          "title": "Nirvana temple",
          "name": "Nirvana temple",
          "desc": "excavations were conducted by the Archaeological Survey of India at Kushinagar. The monuments of Kushinagar are clustered in threedistinct groups comprising the main site at the Nirvana temple, the central Stupa and surrounding monastery of Mathakuar Kot in the south-west and the Ramabhar Stupa, a kilometer to the east. Nirvana Stupa is a huge brickwork stupa, stands at a height of 2.74 mtr. A copper vessel was unearthed at this site. It bore an inscription in ancient Brahmi, which stated that Lord Buddha's remains had been deposited here. Mahaparinirvana Temple houses a 6.10 meter long statue of the reclining Buddha. Carved from Chunar sandstone, the statue represents the dying Buddha reclining on his right side. An inscription below dates the statue to the fifth century. Mathakuar Shrine a stone image of Lord Buddha in the Bhumi Sparsha Mudra (posture touching the earth while sitting) under the Bodhi tree was excavated here. The inscription at the base of the statue dates it to tenth eleventh century. Adjacent to this shrine, ruins of a monastery have also been discovered. Ramabhar Stupa About 1.5 km away from Mahaparinirvana temple, is a large Stupa rising to a height of 15 meters. It marks the site where Lord Buddha was cremated. In ancient Buddhist texts, this stupa has been referred to as Mukut-Bandhan Chaitya Kushinagar is also known as Kasia or Kusinara. The founder of Buddhism, Lord Buddha passed away at this place near the Hiranyavati River and was cremated at the Ramabhar stupa. It was once a celebrated center of the Malla kingdom. Many of its stupas and viharas date back to 230 BCE-413 CE when its prosperity was at the peak. The Mauryan emperor Ashoka added grandeur to this place by getting the magnificent statue of Buddha carved on a single piece of red sandstone. All the three famous Chinese scholar Fa Hien, Huen Tsang, and I-tsing, travellers to India visited Kushinagar. With the decline of Buddhism, however, Kushinagar lost its importance and suffered much neglect. It was only in the nineteenth century that Cunningham excavated many important remnants of the main site such as the Matha Kuar and Ramabhar stupa. Today, people from all over the world visit Kushinagar. Many national and international societies and groups have established their centers here.",
          "img": "",
          "historical_significance": "900-1000",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 28.013372470431417,
          "lon": 81.015270349806
        },
        {
          "id": "frtdovg89",
          "title": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Sravasti",
          "name": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Sravasti",
          "desc": "Sravasti, capital of the ancient Kosala kingdom is sacred to Buddhists because it is here that Lord Buddha performed the greatest of his miracles. These miracles include that of the Buddha creating multiple images of himself, hence forth has been among the favorite themes of Buddhist art. An active centre of Buddhism even in the Buddha\u0092s lifetime, the Buddha himself spent many summers here, and delivered important sermons. It was here that Anathapindaka built a large monastery for the reception of the Buddha in the garden of Prince Jeta. Later, several shrines and other monasteries arose at this sacred spot. Sravasti has been identified with the remains at Saheth - Maheth on the borders of the Gonda and Bahraich districts. Srarvasti also is also important for Jain devotees who visit during the month of Kartik (Oct.-Nov.) to commemorate the bithday of Jain Tirthankar Lord Sambhavnath, when an annual fair is held. Saheth, was the site of the Jetavana monastery. It became an important place of pilgrimage, adorned with numerous shrines, stupas and monasteries. The stupas belong mostly to the Kushana period, while the temples are in the Gupta style. The remains date from the Mauryan era (third century BCE) to the twelfth century CE. One of the earliest stupas. Probably dating to the 3rd century BC contained relics of the Buddha. A colossal statue of the Buddha was also found here which is now preserved in the Indian Museum, Calcutta. The Emperor Ashoka visited Jetavana, and the Chinese pilgrim Hiuen- Tsang mentions two Ashokan pillars at Sravasti.",
          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/Sravasti-gandhakuti700.webp",
          "historical_significance": "",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 27.5083429,
          "lon": 82.0263569
        },
        {
          "id": "z6z302yw4",
          "title": "Dashavatar Temple",
          "name": "Dashavatar Temple",
          "desc": "Deogarh is situated on the right bank of the Betwa river, at the western end of the Lalitpur range of hills. It is of great antiquarian, epigraphical and archaeological importance and has been integral to the history of the Guptas, the Gurjara-Pratiharas, the Gondas, the Muslim rulers of Delhi, the Marathas and the British. It possesses the remains of a fine Vishnu temple dating to the Gupta era, and several Jain temples.The architecture and sculpture of these temples display a high level of craftsmanship. Dashavatar Temple is a fine Gupta temple dedicated to Lord Vishnu and is the earliest known Panchyatan temple in north India. The terraced basement above the high plinth is decorated with sculpted panels. The figures of Ganga and Yamuna adorn the carved doorway leading to the sanctum sanctorum. Three large carved panels along the side walls-the Gajendramoksha panel, the Nar Narayan Tapasya and the Anantshayi Vishnu-depict scenes from Vaishnava mythology. Only the lower portion remains of the spire, which was the most important part of the temple.",
          "img": "/state-images/Uttar_Pradesh/dashavatar-Deogarh.webp",
          "historical_significance": "100-600 CE",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 27.979226029160696,
          "lon": 81.45741993061
        },
        {
          "id": "vh7wbqdzh",
          "title": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Jain Temples",
          "name": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Jain Temples",
          "desc": "The 31 Jain temples are somewhat later in origin that the Vishnu temple, but are no less remarkable. They are situated inside the fort of Kanali on the hills overlooking the Betwa. The temples abound in panels depicting scenes from Jain mythology, Tirthankara images, votive pillars, votive tablets, Jain images visible from all sides and pillars carved with a thousand Jain figures.",
          "img": "",
          "historical_significance": "100-600 CE",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 24.5181986,
          "lon": 78.2460306
        },
        {
          "id": "vuybaf812",
          "title": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Bhitargaon Temple",
          "name": "Uttar Pradesh- Monument- Bhitargaon Temple",
          "desc": "Situated in Ghatampur tehsil, Bhitargaon is a unique architectural specimen \u0096 a brick temple with a shikara belonging to the Gupta period. Built of large bricks and set in mud mortar, the total height of the temple is 15.41m. Inside the temple only the sanctum or garbhagriha and the porch exist. The most remarked feature of the temple is its recessed plan. In contrast to the interior the exterior is decorated with carved brickwork and numerous terracotta panels of skilful workmanship. The importance of the Bhitargaon temple lies in the fact that it is the sole surviving record of this early phase of temple architecture in India.",
          "img": "",
          "historical_significance": "100-600 CE",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 26.658622380435045,
          "lon": 79.93863369514663
        },
