    {
      "id": "west-bengal",
      "name": "West Bengal",
      "geography": {
        "capital": "Kolkata",
        "key_cities": []
      },
      "facts": [
        "**Kantha Embroidery**: Kantha evolved out of necessity to drape or protect against cold. Kontha in Sanskrit means rags. It can rightly be called the recycling art. The precious silks and muslins when became worn-out, women instead of throwing them away, piled them in layers and stitched. The Kantha narrates a story, the emotions and the life of the artist. The process involves laying the worn clothes in layers and stitching them together. Though the stitch used is variations of running stitch, the motifs can range from being simple to very intricate. Apart from floral motifs, kantha also displays animals, birds and"
      ],
      "art_forms": {
        "paintings": [
          {
            "id": "uy04c67a7",
            "title": "Company paintings",
            "name": "Company paintings",
            "desc": "Built entirely of white marble, Victoria Memorial represents a unique combination of classical European architecture and Mughal motifs. The domed and white marble museum sprawls over acres of land and is set in a landscaped garden at the southern side of Kolkata. Designed after the Taj Mahal in Agra, it has a vast collection of pictures, statues, manuscripts, letters etc, relating to the Nawabi and British times in Bengal. The memorial was the brainwave of Lord Curzon, who in 1901, felt that the recently departed Queen Victoria, required a suitable monument in her memory. Completed in 20 years, the Prince of Wales formally inaugurated it in 1921. Set amidst acres of lawns, and fountains the memorial consists of numerous features including Italian-style statues over its entrances, Mughal domes in its corners and tall elegant open curved pillars along its sides. There are about 3000 exhibits in 25 galleries in a chamber beneath the dome. A bronze statue of Queen Victoria sits enthroned at the entrance marble staircase wearing the regal Order of the Star of India. The entrance dome is deeply engraved with the text of Queen Victoria's imperial proclamation speech. Inside the memorial there is a remarkable collection of artifacts depicting British Imperialism. In the Royal Gallery there are many oil paintings illustrating episodes from Queen Victoria's long, eventful life and reign. The memorial is famous for its exhibits of Indian and Western",
            "img": "/state-images/West_Bengal/vic-memorial.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Modern 1900-1947",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "t8n5hnday",
            "title": "Kalighat Paintings",
            "name": "Kalighat Paintings",
            "desc": "Tradition of Kalighat paintings in Bengal has the genesis from the place where the artists originally set up their practice - around the Kalighat temple in Kolkata. Scroll painters-cum-potters migrated from rural Bengal to the city of Calcutta in the nineteenth century and created this art on mill-made paper with watercolours. Kalighat paintings were mainly depictions of Hindu gods and goddesses including their various incarnations (avatars). But gradually the influence expanded and Kalighat paintings became a reflection of the society of their time. The artists used this medium to record their impressions of the dynamic social environment they lived in. Calcutta, the capital of British India served as the inspiration for these artists. They turned their satirical gaze towards a changing society, changing lifestyles and industrial progress. The Indian art scene, in particular, was under the threat of being swept by the wave of westernisation. Decorative Indian Art was being tarnished and corrupted beyond recognition. The survival of traditional Indian arts therefore became a prime concern. Local traditions suddenly assumed vital importance and there was an acute need for protecting, documenting and reviving rural art. This largely led to Kalighat Paintings being given the importance due to them.",
            "img": "/state-images/West_Bengal/kalighat_1.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "stzksvntm",
            "title": "Scroll Paintings",
            "name": "Scroll Paintings",
            "desc": "The craft of scroll painting in West Bengal is traditional and the painters of this type of scroll pat painting are known as patidars. They take a very long old piece of cloth on which they lay black earth paste often mixed with cowdung. When this cloth is dried, a coating of lac is given to stiffen it and fill up the porous surface. The outlines of the figures are in lamp black or red and the bodies are filled with vegetable dyes which retain their brilliance over a long period. The figures are symbolic and bold, vigorous and spontaneous in style.",
            "img": "/state-images/West_Bengal/scroll.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "0dnd53z3c",
            "title": "Paintings- The Bengal School: Artists of Bengal",
            "name": "Paintings- The Bengal School: Artists of Bengal",
            "desc": "The Bengal School was a highly influential style of art that flourished in India during the British Raj in the early 20th century. It was associated with Indian nationalism, but was also promoted and supported by many British arts administrators. EB Havell, a connoisseur and promoter of Indian art, was appointed Principal of Calcutta Art School in 1896. In 1905 Havell persuaded Abanindranath Tagore to join the School as Vice-Principal and together they introduced traditional Indian techniques of art in the school. Nandalal Bose, Surendranath Kar, Asit Kumar Halder, joined as disciples of Abanindranath and helped introduce a new revivalist style of painting. In the nationalistic fervour of the time it gained tremendous popularity and spread all over India. Abanindranath Tagore (1871-1951) Abanindranath Tagore was born in Jorasanko, Kolkata on 7 August 1871. He attended the studio of Charles Palmer, an English painter, for instructions in oil paintings and portraiture. In 1895 he painted the KrishnaLila series, which display a unique blend of both European and Indian, styles. Taikan, a Japanese painter taught Abanindranath the watercolour and wash technique and how to wield the brush with a light touch and of the evocative powers of gestures. He was able to incorporate this learning into his Omar Khayyam series (1906-08). Abanindranath, the painter was established as the creator of a new national vocabulary in art and he helped to regenerate the decadent art and aesthetic scene in India. The Indian Society of Oriental Art was established to promote the Abanindranath-style on the national plane. Abanindranth\u0092s best-known painting, Bharat Mata (mother India),shows her depicted as a young woman, portrayed with four arms like a Goddess holding objects symbolic of India's national aspirations. It was his brush, which first gave convincing proof that the Indian artist had his own contribution to make to the world of painting. Rabindranath Tagore (1861-1941) The Nobel laureate had no formal training in art and yet succeeded in producing more than 2500 works of art within a decade. Over 1500 of them are preserved in Viswa-Bharati, Shantiniketan. Evidently, in his quest for newer forms of expression in terms of line, form and colour, Tagore attempted to do something different from what he did in his literary works. If he sought peace and enlightenment in his songs, he wanted to explore darkness and mystery in his drawings. This puzzled and thrilled the Indian art lovers and connoisseurs. With the passage of time, critics and art lovers discovered in these outpourings of his fanciful mind, a more modern Tagore than they see elsewhere. The works of Jamini Roy (1887-1972) are among the most recognizable of Indian paintings. Roy was a very important Indian artist of the twentieth century. His works have since been declared as national treasures of India and are gaining international acclaim as well. One can see the considerable influence of Bengali tradition in his works, even when the portrayal is of European saints. Roy was trained in his formative period in the Bengal school style of painting and his painting represented as much a revival of folk art as a rebellion against the delicate drawing and hazy colors of the Bengal school. He abandoned the three-dimensional modulation, that is to say, the stress on perspective and light and shade of naturalistic western art. Instead, he took to the two-dimensional format of flat colouring and undulated outline of the folk tradition. He created out of the folk mannerism a more sophisticated and harmonious form of painting which expressed a new kind of simple beauty while retaining a kind of Indianness.",
            "img": "/state-images/West_Bengal/nandlal-bose.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "ecfa79df",
