        "handicrafts": [
          {
            "id": "x31uvxfs7",
            "title": "West Bengal- Handicrafts- Clay and Terracotta",
            "name": "West Bengal- Handicrafts- Clay and Terracotta",
            "desc": "Krishnagar in Nadia district of West Bengal is famous for its sharply chiseled and finely shaped dolls of clay which are moulded by hand and then fired. The dolls have finely detailed depictions of Brahmins, snake charmers, jewellers, women, blacksmiths, potters etc. The toys are extremely shiny because of the application of extract of tamarind seeds.",
            "img": "/state-images-new/West_Bengal/52b6eda453d911c380a94c808c227fb4.jpg",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "u1u8xjew4",
            "title": "West Bengal- Handicrafts- Terracotta",
            "name": "West Bengal- Handicrafts- Terracotta",
            "desc": "West Bengal has some of the largest collection of the finest specimens of temple terracotta panels. Some of the best examples of this craft are seen in Murshidabad, Birbhum, Jessore, Bansberia (Hooghly) and Digha. The use of terracotta plaques, medallions and wall panels as integral part of architecture is undoubtly a speciality of West Bengal. The themes of these art pieces are drawn from epics and old legends. The style of the art is however essentially folk with very expressive traditional designs. The plasticity of the material is very skillfully used.",
            "img": "/state-images-new/West_Bengal/3fa9341cf7d398686b8072b911b11e89.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "4xpenb66k",
            "title": "Sholapith craft",
            "name": "Sholapith craft",
            "desc": "The reed of shola, a herbaceous plant growing wild in the wetlands of West Bengal is used for making decorative objects in the state. The craftsmen known as malakars transform the light and lustrous inner portions of the plant into minutely and delicately worked decorations for the deities particularly that of Durga. They use a special kind of knife called Kath to shave the stalk. Shaving is done very carefully without damaging the stalk as the main motive is to expose the white core. They also design garlands, floral bonquets and mobiles as well as the beautiful headgears (Mukuts) worn by Bengali brides and grooms on their wedding day.",
            "img": "/state-images/West_Bengal/sholapith.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "pldfm1r97",
            "title": "West Bengal- Handicrafts- Stonecraft",
            "name": "West Bengal- Handicrafts- Stonecraft",
            "desc": "Midnapur district is an important traditional region for stoneware with Simulpal as the main centre. The practising craftsmen are karga, bhumif and karmakar. The stone here is called potstone and actually it is phyllite. It is reddish and greenish in one quarry but greyish and blackish in another. The former is more brittle and is more widely used as it is softer. The articles made here are mainly used for daily household needs with the exception of a few decorative items.",
            "img": "/state-images-new/West_Bengal/3fa9341cf7d398686b8072b911b11e89.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "g7pdspusx",
            "title": "West Bengal- Handicrafts- Masks",
            "name": "West Bengal- Handicrafts- Masks",
            "desc": "In West Bengal the wooden masks are an important part of the attire of the Gambhira dancers. The Purulia Chhou masks represent gods and goddesses and mark the beginning of spring when episodes from epics are performed. These simple masks are made of papier mache and have a quality of earthiness about them.",
            "img": "/state-images/West_Bengal/chhaumask.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "132i9cyse",
            "title": "West Bengal- Handicrafts- Baluchar Silk",
            "name": "West Bengal- Handicrafts- Baluchar Silk",
            "desc": "The Baluchar silk sarees originate from a place of the same name in the district of Murshidabad and had its heyday under the patronage of royalty and nobility. Very colourful, a real traditional Baluchar is said to have 17 colours in it with embroidered designs (butis) woven all over. It is a plain woven fabric brocaded with untwisted silk thread with unique designs. The pallu which is the main piece of a large panel with mangoes in the centre is bordered by diverse designs ranging from scenes depicting the royal court to birds, flowers and historical monuments. The sarees are always in dark rich shades, particularly red, purple and browns. The simpler baluchar has small dots or flowers all over with a flowery border and mango patterns (ambi) in the pallu.",
            "img": "/state-images-new/West_Bengal/3fa9341cf7d398686b8072b911b11e89.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "mtxnizj0s",
            "title": "West Bengal- Handicrafts- Durries",
            "name": "West Bengal- Handicrafts- Durries",
            "desc": "In Darjeeling, the local Bhutias traditionally weave durries. Their designs show strong Tibetan influences with dragons, clouds, etc. Around Shantiniketan, where poet Rabindranath Tagore had initiated a revival of handicrafts, interesting experiments were made using jute yarn for the weft and cotton for the warp with local folk designs from the old kantha embroideries. These designs however take on a new look when presented in this broad weave. The loom is a simple bamboo frame and the panja is used to bear the weft thread.",
            "img": "/state-images-new/West_Bengal/3fa9341cf7d398686b8072b911b11e89.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
          {
            "id": "bxkeyg6qn",
            "title": "West Bengal- Handicrafts- Conch shell",
            "name": "West Bengal- Handicrafts- Conch shell",
            "desc": "West Bengal has a heritage of crafts that is based on the resources of the sea and on land locked traditions. In Kolkata and Midnapore, sankharis or conchshell workers practice this age-old craft. With an expertise honed to near perfection by long years of experience, they slice the radiating conchshells with simple hand tools and fashion bangles, bracelets and a range of ornaments. Ritualistically, conchshell bangles, once worn, are not to be discarded by Bengali women during the lifetime of their husbands. Along with shell articles cutlery such as spoons, forks, knives, small plates, and jewellery are also produced.",
            "img": "/state-images-new/West_Bengal/3fa9341cf7d398686b8072b911b11e89.webp",
            "historical_significance": "Contemporary",
            "materials": ""
          },
