        "dance_forms": {
          "folk": [
            {
              "id": "364de322",
              "name": "Chhau (Purulia)- Odisha, Jharkhand (Seraikella Chhau) (Ref: NCERT Textbook hekr1, Ch. 10) and West Bengal",
              "desc": "The Chhau dance of Purulia district is one of the most vibrant and colourful art forms. Emerging from martial practice, the Purulia Chhau is a vigorous form of dance drama that draws its themes from the two great Indian epics, The Ramayana and The Mahabharata.\nMasks and elaborate headgear are the ornamental apparels of the Chhau dancers. The dance is believed to date back to over a century, though the specific date of its origin cannot be definitely ascertained. The dance was patronised by the royalty and landlords of the region. Since its inception, the members of the Mahato, Kurmi, Bhumija, Deowa, Bhuama and Dom communities have sustained this dance form. The dance is an essential part of the Gajan Festival, a festival that celebrates the glories of Lord Shiva. Today, the dance is no longer restricted to one particular time of the year. The Purulia Chhau dance has been influenced by many dances of the district, like the Nata Jawaid Dance, the Mahi Dance and the Nachni Dance. Even two relatively sophisticated dance forms like the Jhumur and Bhadra Jhumur have influenced the Chhau Dance in its tune and rhythm. The accompanying musical instruments include the Dhol, Dhamsa and Shenai. \n\n(Ref: NCERT Textbook fekr1, Ch. 13)",
              "img": "/downloaded_images/b9022cc356ccf737e604d839689d576d.webp",
              "artists": "Rabindranath Tagore - Rabindra Nritya. (Ref: NCERT Textbook gekr1, Ch. 12) \nRabindranath Tagore established one of the first fine arts universities, the Vishwabharati Universityin Shantiniketan in 921. It was instrumental in developing a new dance vocabulary, drawing from our traditional dances that came to be known as Rabindra Nritya. He pioneered professional dance for women."
            }
          ],
          "classical": []
        }
      },
      "monuments": [
        {
          "id": "kptxj6ksw",
          "title": "West Bengal- Monument- Dakshineshwar",
          "name": "West Bengal- Monument- Dakshineshwar",
          "desc": "Dakshineshwar in Hooghly district is known for its Kali temple, being associated with Ramakrishna Paramahamsa in the 19th century who was the spiritual Guru of Swami Vivekananda. Located in nearby Kolkata, is the well known Kalighat temple also dedicated to Kali. The temple although erected in 1855, is representative of Bangla temple architecture, with a cluster of towers, with a central dome towering above the rest, with a two layered roof - one superimposed on the other, with four domes in four corners, with a corridor having curvilinear roof in between each pair of domes and the walls are plastered.",
          "img": "/state-images/West_Bengal/dakshineshwar.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Modern 1850-1900",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 22.6540325,
          "lon": 88.3637533
        },
        {
          "id": "c9j2tdgbq",
          "title": "Terracotta Temples, Bishnupur",
          "name": "Terracotta Temples, Bishnupur",
          "desc": "Bishnupur boasts of thirty temples built between seventeenth and eighteenth century. It is a treasure-trove of terracotta sculptures. The temples are built mostly of brick and at times, of laterite. The architecture is modeled on the pattern of Bengali huts built of bamboo and mud and thatched roofed. For almost a thousand years it was the capital of the Malla kings of Mallabhum, of which Bankura was a part. The temples are dedicated to Radha and Krishna. Here, royal architects and craftsmen of Bengal have poured all their talents into the red laterite soil found here, and the result was one of the most beautiful cities in the world of its time built of terracotta. Apart from the temples, terracotta pottery, artifacts and even jewellery from Vishnupur is famous. The potters here derive their inspiration from the glorious history of kings, soldiers and wars. Rasmancha is the oldest brick temple having a unique style with a cubic shrine, pillared corridors on three sides and a pyramidal roof. The temples built after this Pancharatna Temple , the Radha Shyama Temple and the Keshta Raya Temple are the best examples of terracotta architecture. The temple built of bricks has an exterior and interior clad with high quality, densely sculpted, terracotta panels. It is a Pancharatna with a main tower and four small towers in the four corners. The temple has a square plan with porches on all four sides and a three-arch entrance, on each of the four sides. The ceiling is is in the form of a dome with a terracotta finish having Islamic influence with a terracotta finish that is not seen in any other temple. Most of the sculptures are of Krishna. There are Rasa mandalas with Krishna, Radha and gopis. Jorebangla Temple has two traditional Bengali huts placed together and the sloping split roof called Bangaldar roof. Madanmohan temple built in 1694 CE in the Ekaratna style, a square flat-roofed building with carved cornices, surmounted by a pinnacle. Impressive carvings on the walls depict scenes from the Ramayana, Mahabharata and the Puranas. The temples at Bishnupur are mostly of Ekaratna type with a single tower upon a sloping roof and a square cell (Garbhagriha) flanked by a porch on each side with three cusped arches.",
          "img": "/state-images/West_Bengal/bishnupur-1.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Medieval 1600-1650",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 23.366545749967475,
          "lon": 86.7479961000165
        },
        {
          "id": "lcykh58e6",
          "title": "West Bengal- Monument- Shantiniketan",
          "name": "West Bengal- Monument- Shantiniketan",
          "desc": "Shantiniketan, was founded in 1863 by Maharishi Devendranath Tagore and it was his son, Rabindranath who converted it into an experimental open-air school with just five students in 1901. Gradually it developed into the Vishva Bharati University which became an important centre of learning including arts. The Vishva Bharati campus is an interesting synthesis of murals, frescoes, paintings and sculptures. Rabindranath Tagore, Nandlal Bose, Ramkinkar Baij, amongst other prominent personalities in education built this educational movement. The Uttarayan complex where Tagore lived has a number of architecturally unique buildings such as the Udayana, Konark, Shyamali, Punascha and Udichi. About 3 km from Shantiniketan is Sriniketan which was Tagore\u0092s vision of rural expansion. Sriniketan focuses on agriculture, adult education, village welfare, cottage industries and handicrafts. Rural co-operative stores sell the handicrafts produced in neighbouring villages, such as - batik, kantha embroidery, weaving, bell metal products, dhokra metalware, leather goods and terracotta pottery.etc.",
          "img": "/state-images/West_Bengal/rabindranath-tagore.webp",
          "historical_significance": "Modern 1850-1900",
          "materials": "",
          "lat": 23.682708,
          "lon": 87.6880296
        },
