    {
      "id": "9ae1c77a",
      "name": "Odissi",
      "desc": "Odissi, dance form originates from Odisha in eastern India. Various sculptural postures from the temples of Odisha are depicted in this dance form. The dance is smooth and flowing, with slow turns and graceful steps. This form has been derived from Maharis\u2015Devadasis of Odisha and Gotipua\u2009\u2015group of boy dancers, dressed as girls who dance for Lord Vishnu. A special feature is the tribhanga pose, where the body bends in three places the head, torso, and hip.  Dancers often stand in a square-shaped and half-sitting pose called chauka. Costumes are made from traditional fabrics called pattasari (silk) with step fanshaped pleats and beautiful silver filigree jewellery and a flowerlike headpiece called tahiya.",
      "img": "/downloaded_images/6f94f6cd19402d34a90605b75c34ecc2.webp",
      "images": [
        "/downloaded_images/6f94f6cd19402d34a90605b75c34ecc2.webp",
        "/downloaded_images/4e5752c6cc588c2d66c224957f46a8f8.webp",
        "/downloaded_images/6e67712e1d417ad3b8e384ebc6598e3d.webp",
        "/downloaded_images/2b0bef192c96cb3e871c88805f8b7da2.webp"
      ],
      "artists": "Kelucharan Mohapatra\n \nHe was an undisputed master performing artist, choreographer, teacher, and percussionist (pakhawaj) of Odissi Dance. He was the recipient of Padma Vibhushan.",
      "artists_images": [
        "/downloaded_images/304ebcc51d66aa0801f601801855b48c.webp",
